&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Session 000 (bootstrap) is already finished. Session 001 will target HF-1: the stub of the Gradle plugin and the AsciidoctorJ integration.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_why_it_works&quot;&gt;Why It Works&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Three reasons :&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;olist arabic&quot;&gt;
&lt;ol class=&quot;arabic&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;HyperFrames is &quot;built for agents&quot;&lt;/strong&gt;. My pipeline relies on AI agents.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;(planner-gradle + deepseek) that generate content. HyperFrames accepts Raw HTML — the format that LLMs master best. No React, no of JSX, no learning curve for the agent.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;olist arabic&quot;&gt;
&lt;ol class=&quot;arabic&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The AsciiDoc DSL is natural&lt;/strong&gt;. My whole ecosystem speaks AsciiDoc.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;slider-gradle, codex-gradle, training-gradle — all consume`.adoc`. Add custom blocks`[hyperframes-composition]`is an extension logical, not a rupture&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;olist arabic&quot;&gt;
&lt;ol class=&quot;arabic&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The Node.js bridge is a proven pattern&lt;/strong&gt;. ProcessBuilder outside the JVM,&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is what I already do for PlantUML, Graphviz, Piper. Add HyperFrames It changes nothing about the architecture — it’s just another external tool, driven by Gradle.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;admonitionblock note&quot;&gt;
&lt;table&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;icon&quot;&gt;
&lt;i class=&quot;fa icon-note&quot; title=&quot;ℹ️&quot;&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The real strength of the pattern is that there is &lt;strong&gt;no&lt;/strong&gt; npm dependency in the Gradle build. The plugin does not import any Node.js code. It executes a shell command. If HyperFrames evolves or breaks, the plugin is not coupled — it&amp;#8217;s enough to update the command.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_conclusion_the_missing_link&quot;&gt;Conclusion: The Missing Link&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With hyperframes-gradle, my video pipeline is complete. I have:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;ulist&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;interactive slides&lt;/strong&gt; (slider-gradle)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;review capsules&lt;/strong&gt; (capsule-gradle)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;standalone videos&lt;/strong&gt; (hyperframes-gradle)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Three formats. One single source file: AsciiDoc.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The next goal is HF-1: compile the plugin and generate a first HTML HyperFrames from an annotated AsciiDoc document. The session 001 is already framed.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;hyperframes-gradle exists. The governance is in place. The backlog is written. The architecture is documented. The plugin is born.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
