&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#_the_dag_contract_who_matters_whom&quot;&gt;The DAG Contract: Who Matters Whom&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#_what_we_gain_and_what_we_dont_gain&quot;&gt;What We Gain (And What We Don&amp;#8217;t Gain)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#_perspectives_the_blog_as_a_public_knowledge_graph&quot;&gt;Perspectives: The Blog as a Public Knowledge Graph&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#_conclusion_the_file_system_knows_what_the_template_ignores&quot;&gt;Conclusion : The file system knows what the template ignores&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#_references&quot;&gt;References&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div id=&quot;preamble&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;title&quot;&gt;reading time : 14 minutes&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;You are reading an article about the integration of pgvector with LangChain4j. At the bottom of the page, JBake suggests &#39;Related Articles&#39;. You click. It&amp;#8217;s an article about&amp;#8230;&amp;#8203; the configuration of Kitty Terminal. The only point they have in common? They were published less than fifteen days apart. This is not a recommendation. It is a calendar disguised as editorial.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_the_scene_tuesday_may_12_530_pm&quot;&gt;The Scene: Tuesday May 12, 5:30 PM&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I am rereading one of my articles — &lt;a href=&quot;0106_knowledge_graph_outil_comprehension_codebase_post.html&quot;&gt;the one on the Knowledge Graph as a tool for codebase comprehension&lt;/a&gt;. The content is dense: nodes, edges, communities, PlantUML, onboarding. An article 15-minute reading technique that mobilizes`graphify-gradle`, &lt;code&gt;plantuml-gradle&lt;/code&gt;, and the concepts of graph topology.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;At the bottom of the page, the &quot;Related articles&quot; suggest:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;olist arabic&quot;&gt;
&lt;ol class=&quot;arabic&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;An article on Firebase Contact Form (0113)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;An article on the Eager/Lazy mechanism (0108)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;An article on Gradle script → plugin migration (0102)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;An article on the cumulative Ollama Pro subscriptions (0120)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Three of these four articles have &lt;strong&gt;no&lt;/strong&gt; relation to the Knowledge Graph. They are there because they are the latest published — temporal proximity, not semantic neighborhood.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I am looking at the template`post.thyme`:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-xhtml hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;xhtml&quot;&gt;&amp;lt;!-- Articles connexes (liens internes SEO) --&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;th:block th:each=&quot;post,postStat : ${published_posts}&quot;&amp;gt;
    &amp;lt;th:block th:if=&quot;${!post.uri.equals(content.uri) and postStat.index lt 4}&quot;&amp;gt;
        ...
    &amp;lt;/th:block&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;/th:block&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;`published_posts`is a chronological list.`postStat.index lt 4`takes the The first four that are not the current post. That&amp;#8217;s it. No logic of similarity. No notion of content. Just a loop`for`disguised as recommendation.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;admonitionblock warning&quot;&gt;
&lt;table&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;icon&quot;&gt;
&lt;i class=&quot;fa icon-warning&quot; title=&quot;⚠️&quot;&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It&amp;#8217;s not a JBake bug. It&amp;#8217;s the default behavior of everything. static site generator: the list of posts is flat and ordered by date. The template does what it can with what it has.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;But that&amp;#8217;s not a reason to accept it.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_the_diagnosis_three_types_of_relations_that_no_template_sees&quot;&gt;The Diagnosis: Three Types of Relations That No Template Sees&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;My article corpus — 23 posts in 4 months — has a rich structure that the chronological template completely overwhelms:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;olist arabic&quot;&gt;
&lt;ol class=&quot;arabic&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Explicit references&lt;/strong&gt; : I use`xref:`massively in my articles.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Article 0122 references 0106 (knowledge graph) and 0116 (compartmentalization epistemic). These links are editorial &lt;strong&gt;hard linking&lt;/strong&gt; — I&amp;#8217;ve deliberately decided to connect these concepts&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;olist arabic&quot;&gt;
&lt;ol class=&quot;arabic&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Shared Tags&lt;/strong&gt; : each article has some`:jbake-tags:`. The article on&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Graphify + PlantUML (0105) has`gradle, graphify, plantuml, knowledge-graph`. The article about the Knowledge Graph (0106) has &lt;code&gt;knowledge-graph, graphify, plantuml&lt;/code&gt;. Three tags in common out of seven — 43% overlap.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;olist arabic&quot;&gt;
&lt;ol class=&quot;arabic&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Co‑occurring named entities&lt;/strong&gt; : « pgvector », « RAG », « embedding&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;appear together in four different articles. « LangChain4j », “Ollama”, “Gradle plugin” in six others. These are not tags declared — these are &lt;strong&gt;patterns émergents&lt;/strong&gt; of the corpus that only an NLP can detect.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;imageblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;img src=&quot;./images/trois-couches-relation-articles.svg&quot; alt=&quot;trois couches relation articles&quot; width=&quot;1746&quot; height=&quot;5492&quot;&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Today, my site does not use &lt;strong&gt;any&lt;/strong&gt; of these three layers. It uses layer zero: the insertion order in a Java list&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_the_solution_a_gradle_pipeline_not_a_hot_llm_call&quot;&gt;The solution: a Gradle pipeline, not a hot LLM call&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The first temptation would be to call an LLM at bake time: « For this article, finds the three most similar articles in the corpus.  Don&amp;#8217;t do that.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;admonitionblock important&quot;&gt;
&lt;table&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;icon&quot;&gt;
&lt;i class=&quot;fa icon-important&quot; title=&quot;❗&quot;&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;An LLM call to each`./gradlew bake`costs time, money, and introduces non-determinism into your build. The LLM&amp;#8217;s response can to switch between two builds without the content having changed. Your CI becomes non-reproducible, your tests become flaky.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The solution is deterministic: a Gradle pipeline that pre-calculates the graph of similarity and stores it in`graph.json`. The template reads the result — he never triggers a calculation.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect2&quot;&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;_architecture_bakery_imports_graphify_not_engine&quot;&gt;Architecture : Bakery imports Graphify, not engine&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is the most important architectural point. The temptation would be to wire the collaboration in`engine/build.gradle.kts`engine applies graphify for the scan, bakery for the bake, and engine makes the bridge between the two.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It&amp;#8217;s an anti-pattern. Engine is a &lt;strong&gt;consumer terminal&lt;/strong&gt; — it applies plugins, it does not implement business logic. The rule is: &lt;strong&gt;The burden of proof lies with the proprietary plugin.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;imageblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;img src=&quot;./images/bakery-import-graphify.svg&quot; alt=&quot;bakery import graphify&quot; width=&quot;1749&quot; height=&quot;5700&quot;&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;title&quot;&gt;Figure 1. Architecture correct — Bakery imports Graphify&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The DAG contract is respected: bakery (N2) imports graphify (N0), N2 &amp;gt; N0, no violation. Engine (N3) imports bakery (N2), N3 &amp;gt; N2, OK.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Engine has &lt;strong&gt;no&lt;/strong&gt; line of code that references`graph.json`, &lt;code&gt;relatedPosts&lt;/code&gt;, or any other notion of similarity. He applies bakery, period. The collaboration is internal to bakery.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect2&quot;&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;_the_pipeline_scan_graph_template&quot;&gt;The Pipeline: Scan → Graph → Template&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Here is the complete three-step pipeline:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;olist arabic&quot;&gt;
&lt;ol class=&quot;arabic&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Scan&lt;/strong&gt; (graphify):`graphify-plugin`scans the workspace. In its&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;current form, it already detects the`xref:`between`.adoc`and the presnted in`graph.json`like edges of type`reference`.&lt;br&gt;
We enrich it for editorial content: * Parse JBake metadata (&lt;code&gt;:jbake-tags:&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;:jbake-description:&lt;/code&gt;) of each`.adoc`of the blog * Calculate tag co-occurrences → edges`tag_cooccurrence`with weight * Extract named entities from descriptions via TF-IDF → edges`entity_overlap` * Injects a section`blog_articles`in the`graph.json`existing&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-kotlin hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;kotlin&quot;&gt;// Extrait de l&#39;enrichissement graphify pour le blog
fun enrichBlogSection(graphJson: File, blogDir: File): GraphJson {
    val articles = blogDir.listFiles { f -&amp;gt; f.extension == &quot;adoc&quot; }
        .map { parseJbakeMetadata(it) }

    val nodes = articles.map { ArticleNode(it.slug, it.title, it.tags) }
    val edges = mutableListOf&amp;lt;GraphEdge&amp;gt;()

    // Couche 1 : xref (déjà fait par scanWorkspace)

    // Couche 2 : co-occurrences de tags
    for (a in articles) {
        for (b in articles) {
            if (a.slug == b.slug) continue
            val common = a.tags.intersect(b.tags)
            if (common.isNotEmpty()) {
                edges.add(GraphEdge(
                    source = a.slug,
                    target = b.slug,
                    type = &quot;tag_cooccurrence&quot;,
                    weight = common.size.toDouble() / (a.tags.size + b.tags.size)
                ))
            }
        }
    }

    return graphJson.copy(
        blogArticles = BlogSection(nodes, edges)
    )
}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;olist arabic&quot;&gt;
&lt;ol class=&quot;arabic&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Bake&lt;/strong&gt; (bakery): at the moment of the`./gradlew bake`, `BakeryPlugin`bed&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;`graph.json`and resolves related articles for each post.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-kotlin hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;kotlin&quot;&gt;// BakeryPlugin — résolution des articles connexes
fun resolveRelatedPosts(
    currentSlug: String,
    graph: GraphJson,
    maxResults: Int = 4
): List&amp;lt;RelatedPost&amp;gt; {
    val edges = graph.blogArticles.edges
        .filter { it.source == currentSlug || it.target == currentSlug }

    return edges
        .sortedByDescending { it.weight }
        .take(maxResults)
        .map { edge -&amp;gt;
            val relatedSlug = if (edge.source == currentSlug) edge.target else edge.source
            graph.blogArticles.nodes.first { it.slug == relatedSlug }
        }
}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;olist arabic&quot;&gt;
&lt;ol class=&quot;arabic&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Template&lt;/strong&gt; (&lt;code&gt;post.thyme&lt;/code&gt;) : the JBake model now receives a map&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;structured instead of a flat chronological list.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-xhtml hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;xhtml&quot;&gt;&amp;lt;!-- Articles connexes basés sur le Knowledge Graph --&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;th:block th:if=&quot;${relatedPosts != null and !relatedPosts.empty}&quot;&amp;gt;
    &amp;lt;section class=&quot;mt-5 pt-4 border-top&quot;&amp;gt;
        &amp;lt;h2 class=&quot;h4 mb-3&quot;&amp;gt;Articles connexes&amp;lt;/h2&amp;gt;
        &amp;lt;th:block th:each=&quot;related : ${relatedPosts}&quot;&amp;gt;
            &amp;lt;div class=&quot;mb-2&quot;&amp;gt;
                &amp;lt;a th:href=&quot;${content.rootpath} + ${related.uri}&quot;
                   th:text=&quot;${related.title}&quot; class=&quot;fw-semibold&quot;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt;
                &amp;lt;br/&amp;gt;
                &amp;lt;small class=&quot;text-muted&quot;&amp;gt;
                    &amp;lt;th:block th:each=&quot;reason,iterStat : ${related.reasons}&quot;&amp;gt;
                        &amp;lt;span class=&quot;badge bg-light text-dark&quot;
                              th:text=&quot;${reason}&quot;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt;
                    &amp;lt;/th:block&amp;gt;
                &amp;lt;/small&amp;gt;
            &amp;lt;/div&amp;gt;
        &amp;lt;/th:block&amp;gt;
    &amp;lt;/section&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;/th:block&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The template is the same — it doesn&amp;#8217;t know &lt;strong&gt;where&lt;/strong&gt; the data comes from. Only the contract between bakery and JBake has changed:`published_posts`(list chronological) becomes`relatedPosts`(graph-weighted map).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;admonitionblock note&quot;&gt;
&lt;table&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;icon&quot;&gt;
&lt;i class=&quot;fa icon-note&quot; title=&quot;ℹ️&quot;&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The badge « raison » (e.g:`xref 0122`, &lt;code&gt;tag:gradle&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;cluster:pgvector-rag&lt;/code&gt;) explains to the reader &lt;strong&gt;why&lt;/strong&gt; this article is related. It is not just transparency — it&amp;#8217;s pedagogy about the topology of your own content.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect2&quot;&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;_fallback_chronological&quot;&gt;Fallback Chronological&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Si `graph.json`is absent (local build without prior scan, first deployment, CI that has not yet integrated the graphify scan), the template must degrade gracefully:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-kotlin hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;kotlin&quot;&gt;fun resolveRelatedPosts(currentSlug: String, graph: GraphJson?): List&amp;lt;RelatedPost&amp;gt; {
    if (graph != null &amp;amp;&amp;amp; graph.blogArticles != null) {
        return resolveFromGraph(currentSlug, graph)
    }
    // Fallback chronologique — même comportement qu&#39;aujourd&#39;hui
    logger.warn(&quot;[bakery] graph.json absent — fallback chronologique&quot;)
    return resolveFromChronology(currentSlug)
}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The default behavior is identical to the current behavior. The recommendation engine is a &lt;strong&gt;progressive improvement&lt;/strong&gt; — not a breaking change.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_emergent_ontology_when_articles_cluster_without_knowing_each_other&quot;&gt;Emergent Ontology: When Articles Cluster Without Knowing Each Other&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The most interesting layer is the third: the emergent ontology Articles that are not cited, that do not have the same tags, but who &lt;strong&gt;are talking about the same thing&lt;/strong&gt; without realizing it&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Let&amp;#8217;s take a concrete example. Three articles from my corpus:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;olist arabic&quot;&gt;
&lt;ol class=&quot;arabic&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;0119 — Benchmark DGX Spark vs Cloud Subscription LLM&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;0121 — Gradle Plugin to Pilot Two Ollama Instances Pro&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;0122 — Efficiency Ratio 27x 45x Fleet AI Experts&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;These three articles have &lt;strong&gt;no&lt;/strong&gt; xref between them. Their tags do not They only overlap by 20% (&#39;ollama&#39;, &#39;llm&#39; common). But a lightweight NLP about the descriptions reveals an obvious cluster: “cost”, “subscription”,  cloud », « GPU », « API key », « Ollama Pro », « efficiency », « ratio ».&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They form an ontological cluster: &lt;strong&gt;the economy of self-hosted LLM vs cloud&lt;/strong&gt;. This cluster is not declared anywhere. It emerges from the corpus.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;imageblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;img src=&quot;./images/cluster-ontologique-exemple.svg&quot; alt=&quot;cluster ontologique exemple&quot; width=&quot;697&quot; height=&quot;405&quot;&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This ontological cluster becomes a composite edge in`graph.json`:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-json hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;json&quot;&gt;{
  &quot;source&quot;: &quot;0119-benchmark-dgx-spark&quot;,
  &quot;target&quot;: &quot;cluster:economie-llm&quot;,
  &quot;type&quot;: &quot;entity_cluster&quot;,
  &quot;weight&quot;: 0.73,
  &quot;metadata&quot;: {
    &quot;clusterLabel&quot;: &quot;Économie LLM Self-Hosted vs Cloud&quot;,
    &quot;commonEntities&quot;: [&quot;coût&quot;, &quot;GPU&quot;, &quot;abonnement&quot;, &quot;Ollama Pro&quot;, &quot;ratio&quot;],
    &quot;articlesInCluster&quot;: [
      &quot;0119-benchmark-dgx-spark&quot;,
      &quot;0121-ollama-pro-deux-instances&quot;,
      &quot;0122-ratio-efficacite-flotte-experts&quot;
    ]
  }
}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;NLP is intentionally lightweight — TF-IDF + cosine similarity on the descriptions. No need for BERT, no need for a language model The corpus has 23 articles, the similarity matrix fits in a 50 KB JSON file.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;admonitionblock tip&quot;&gt;
&lt;table&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;icon&quot;&gt;
&lt;i class=&quot;fa icon-tip&quot; title=&quot;💡&quot;&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The power of NLP does not come from the sophistication of the algorithm, but of the &lt;strong&gt;corpus size&lt;/strong&gt; and of the &lt;strong&gt;quality of the descriptions&lt;/strong&gt;. One `:jbake-description:`well written of 150 characters contains more of signal for TF-IDF that an entire article of 3000 words&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_the_dag_contract_who_matters_whom&quot;&gt;The DAG Contract: Who Matters Whom&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It is where architectural discipline pays off. The DAG N0→N3 defined in`engine/build.gradle.kts`gives a simple rule: No project imports a higher‑level project.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;table class=&quot;tableblock frame-all grid-all stretch&quot;&gt;
&lt;colgroup&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 18.1818%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 18.1818%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 18.1818%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 18.1818%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 27.2728%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;/colgroup&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;Consumer plugin&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;Plugin imported&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;Consumer level&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;Imported level&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;Valid ?&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;bakery-gradle&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;graphify-gradle&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;N2&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;N0&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;✅ N2 &amp;gt; N0 — OK&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;engine&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;bakery-gradle&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;N3&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;N2&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;✅ N3 &amp;gt; N2 — OK&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;engine&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;graphify-gradle&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;N3&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;N0&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;✅ Technique OK, but &lt;strong&gt;conceptually wrong&lt;/strong&gt; — the collaboration is internal to bakery&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Engine applies bakery. Bakery applies graphify. That&amp;#8217;s all. If one day I want to add the vector store codebase (N1) for research. cross-corpus semantics, bakery imports it too. Engine does not change.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;admonitionblock important&quot;&gt;
&lt;table&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;icon&quot;&gt;
&lt;i class=&quot;fa icon-important&quot; title=&quot;❗&quot;&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The pattern is: &lt;strong&gt;the N2 plugin is the hub of its own dependencies&lt;/strong&gt;. Engine N3 is not a hub — it&amp;#8217;s a terminal that applies hubs.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_what_we_gain_and_what_we_dont_gain&quot;&gt;What We Gain (And What We Don&amp;#8217;t Gain)&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The main benefit is editorial, not technical:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;table class=&quot;tableblock frame-all grid-all stretch&quot;&gt;
&lt;colgroup&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 50%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 50%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;/colgroup&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;Before&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;After&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;Related articles = 4 latest posts&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;Related articles = top 4 by weight in the Knowledge Graph&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;Reader reads RAG → Kitty Terminal recommendation&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;Reader reads RAG → recommendation pgvector, chunking, vector store&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;Zero transparency on the why&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;Badge`xref 0122`, &lt;code&gt;tag:gradle&lt;/code&gt;, `cluster:rag`visible&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;JBake standard, zero effort, zero value&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;Deterministic, reproducible, testable Gradle pipeline&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;No learning curve for the reader&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;The reader discovers the &lt;strong&gt;topology&lt;/strong&gt; of my content&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;What we &lt;strong&gt;don&amp;#8217;t&lt;/strong&gt; win:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;ulist&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It is not a « true » recommendation engine (no collaborative&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;filtering, no A/B testing, no feedback loop)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;ulist&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The quality depends on the richness of the JBake metadata — if your&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;descriptions are empty, TF-IDF sees nothing&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;ulist&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;NLP is offline — new articles are only clustered up to&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;next scan (it&amp;#8217;s good : the build remains deterministic)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_perspectives_the_blog_as_a_public_knowledge_graph&quot;&gt;Perspectives: The Blog as a Public Knowledge Graph&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This feature opens up a broader perspective: what if`cheroliv.com` becoming himself a &lt;strong&gt;knowledge graph navigable&lt;/strong&gt; ?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Articles are nodes. Tags are communities. xref are edges. The reader no longer reads an isolated article — he navigates in a &lt;strong&gt;knowledge graph&lt;/strong&gt; whose current article is the entry point.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Imagine a home page that does not display the chronological list of the latest posts, but a &lt;strong&gt;map of the corpus&lt;/strong&gt;: ontological clusters, pivot articles (those with the most edges), reading paths If you liked the article about Graphify, read next that on the Knowledge Graph as a comprehension tool&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It&amp;#8217;s no longer a blog. It&amp;#8217;s a &lt;strong&gt;semantic atlas&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;And it&amp;#8217;s not science fiction. The`graph.json`already exists He/She is missing only the navigation interface.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_conclusion_the_file_system_knows_what_the_template_ignores&quot;&gt;Conclusion : The file system knows what the template ignores&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;What I designed this Tuesday night is the replacement of a heuristic lazy (chronological order) by a faithful representation of the structure of my corpus (the Knowledge Graph).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The template`post.thyme`doesn&amp;#8217;t change. What changes is what we feeds him/her. Before: a Java list ordered by`date`. After: a weighted graph derived from three layers of analysis — xrefs, co-occurrences of tags, and emerging ontology by NLP.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The loop is closed. graphify scans the workspace. Bakery reads the graph and feeds the template. The reader sees recommendations relevant. And engine — the conductor — doesn&amp;#8217;t even know that All that exists.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;That&amp;#8217;s it, good architecture. Each plugin does one thing. And the consumer terminal does not need to know how.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
