&lt;ul class=&quot;sectlevel1&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#_the_problem_why_fine_tune_and_not_rag&quot;&gt;The problem: why fine-tune and not RAG?&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#_the_idea_a_fleet_of_experts_not_a_single_model&quot;&gt;The idea: a fleet of experts, not a single model&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#_the_codebase_under_the_microscope_7900_files_144000_lines&quot;&gt;The codebase under the microscope: 7,900 files, 144,000 lines&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div id=&quot;preamble&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;title&quot;&gt;reading time : 22 minutes&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;When using an LLM for everyday software development, you always end up hitting the same wall: the model doesn&amp;#8217;t know &lt;strong&gt;your&lt;/strong&gt; codebase. It doesn&amp;#8217;t know that you use such pattern ofhttps://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graphe_orient%C3%A9_acyclique[DAG], such a Gradle convention, such an epistemological stratification in yourhttps://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/AsciiDoc[.adoc]. Then we inject context into it, we repeat, we correct, we iterate. And time — and money — pass.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;So I bluntly asked: how much would a team of fine‑tuned models on my workspace cost, capable of spitting out code in my style without needing to explain it to them each time? And most importantly: how does this approach compare to classic proprietary solutions — Claude, GPT, Gemini, Grok?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The response surprised me. Much less than I had imagined. And the cost‑effectiveness ratio against the sector giants is outrageous. Here is the full detail.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_the_problem_why_fine_tune_and_not_rag&quot;&gt;The problem: why fine-tune and not RAG?&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;RAG is good. &lt;a href=&quot;0106_knowledge_graph_outil_comprehension_codebase_post.html&quot;&gt;My knowledge graph&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=&quot;0116_compartimentage_epistemique_automatise_llm_post.html&quot;&gt;my epistemic compartmentalization&lt;/a&gt; work. But RAG has a fundamental flaw: for each query, you must re-inject the relevant context. Your prompt gets longer, your latency explodes, and the model never &lt;strong&gt;internalizes&lt;/strong&gt; your patterns — it reads them each time as an external document.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Fine-tuning is muscle memory. The model &lt;strong&gt;knows&lt;/strong&gt; that you are writing.&lt;code&gt;build.gradle.kts`with tasks recorded via`tasks.register&lt;/code&gt;, that your`AGENTS.adoc`follow a standardized syntax, that your workspace is structured into boroughs. He doesn&amp;#8217;t need to be reminded.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;admonitionblock note&quot;&gt;
&lt;table&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;icon&quot;&gt;
&lt;i class=&quot;fa icon-note&quot; title=&quot;ℹ️&quot;&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Fine-tuning is not a replacement for RAG. It is a complementary layer. RAG remains relevant for data that changes frequently (logs, tickets, recent commits). Fine-tuning is for long-term &lt;strong&gt;structural&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;stylistic&lt;/strong&gt; knowledge.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_the_idea_a_fleet_of_experts_not_a_single_model&quot;&gt;The idea: a fleet of experts, not a single model&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A single model fine-tuned on all my workspace is already good. But it&amp;#8217;s sub-optimal. My workspace covers very different domains:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;ulist&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Gradle plugins (Kotlin, convention plugins, composite builds)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;DAG engine (Kotlin, planning, execution)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;LLM Agents (Python, LangChain4j, RAG)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Architectural documentation (AsciiDoc, governance)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Infra/DevOps (Docker, GitHub Actions, CI/CD)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A single model should be good everywhere, excellent nowhere. The solution? A&lt;strong&gt;fleet of specialized models&lt;/strong&gt;, with an orchestrator that routes each request to the right expert.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
