&lt;div id=&quot;preamble&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;title&quot;&gt;reading time : 14 minutes&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Two Pro subscriptions, a single machine, a single bank card, and zero network conflict. How Docker allowed me to double my cloud inference capacity without buying a second PC.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_introduction&quot;&gt;Introduction&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I&amp;#8217;ve been using Ollama Pro for several months to power my OpenCode sessions with cloud models. The thing is, even with a Pro subscription, you quickly hit rate limiting when you launch several agents in parallel. The obvious solution: a second subscription.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;But there, it&amp;#8217;s the drama.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Ollama identifies each machine by a unique SSH key — its famous &lt;strong&gt;Device Key&lt;/strong&gt;. Two instances on the same OS would share the same identity, and it would be impossible to link two Pro accounts to the same device. And as you might have guessed, I wasn&amp;#8217;t going to buy a second laptop just for that.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The solution: cheat. Make Ollama believe it&amp;#8217;s running on two different machines, when they actually share the same CPU, the same RAM, and the same network connection. Docker will give us a perfect isolation bubble.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;Failed to generate image: PlantUML preprocessing failed: [From &amp;lt;input&amp;gt; (line 11) ]

