&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_why_two_times_the_same_thing&quot;&gt;Why two times the same thing?&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Before calling me mentally ill, let me explain the use case.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With a single Pro subscription, I can launch a model like`deepseek-v4-pro:cloud`in an OpenCode session. The problem arises when I want &lt;strong&gt;two&lt;/strong&gt; simultaneous sessions. The rate‑limiting quotas cause the second session to lag or be outright rejected.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With two independent Pro subscriptions :&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;ulist&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Session OpenCode 1 → Pro Account A`localhost:11434`)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Session OpenCode 2 → Account Pro B`localhost:11435`)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Each session has its own quota, its own context, and does not interfere with the other. It&amp;#8217;s human multi-processing.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;admonitionblock important&quot;&gt;
&lt;table&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;icon&quot;&gt;
&lt;i class=&quot;fa icon-important&quot; title=&quot;❗&quot;&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Two Pro subscriptions = two different emails. The same credit card works — Ollama&amp;#8217;s payment system does not block multiple subscriptions from the same payment method. I checked.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_the_heart_of_the_problem_the_device_key&quot;&gt;The Heart of the Problem: The Device Key&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;When you install Ollama, an ed25519 SSH key pair is generated in the identity directory :&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-bash hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;bash&quot;&gt;$ cat /usr/share/ollama/.ollama/id_ed25519.pub
ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAI...&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This key is uploaded to Ollama&amp;#8217;s servers during the`ollama signin`. It is she who says &#39;this machine belongs to a Pro account&#39;. If your two instances share the same file`id_ed25519`, they share the same identity. Game over.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The trick: give our second instance its own identity directory, isolated in a Docker volume.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_setup_step_by_step&quot;&gt;Setup — Step by Step&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect2&quot;&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;_prerequisite&quot;&gt;Prerequisite&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;ulist&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A functional native Ollama installation (official script`curl | sh`)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Docker installed and functional&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Portainer or docker-compose for deployment&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Two Ollama accounts with two distinct emails&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I have used the version`0.20.2`— that provided by the official script and the corresponding Docker image.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect2&quot;&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;_step_1_create_the_volume&quot;&gt;Step 1: Create the Volume&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A simple directory on the host will serve as a persistent volume for the identity of instance B:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-bash hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;bash&quot;&gt;mkdir -p ~/ollama-b-data&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This folder will be mounted in the Docker container as`/root/.ollama`, where Ollama stores its identity keys, its history, and its models.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect2&quot;&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;_step_2_run_the_docker_container&quot;&gt;Step 2: Run the Docker Container&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-yaml hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;yaml&quot;&gt;# docker-compose.yml
services:
  ollama-instance-b:
    image: ollama/ollama:0.20.2
    container_name: ollama-instance-b
    ports:
      - &quot;11435:11434&quot;
    volumes:
      - /home/cheroliv/ollama-b-data:/root/.ollama
    environment:
      - OLLAMA_HOST=0.0.0.0
    restart: always&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;What is happening here:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;ulist&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The port&lt;strong&gt;11435&lt;/strong&gt;of the host is mapped onto the&lt;strong&gt;11434&lt;/strong&gt;internal to the container. Thus, the Docker instance listens on`:11435`without conflict with the native instance that squats`:11434`.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The volume`~/ollama-b-data`is mounted on`/root/.ollama`— this is where the SSH identity will be stored.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;`OLLAMA_HOST=0.0.0.0`allows the container to accept external connections.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I have deployed this stack via Portainer, but a simple`docker compose up -d`works just as well.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect2&quot;&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;_step_3_retrieve_the_public_key&quot;&gt;Step 3: Retrieve the Public Key&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Since the container is empty on first startup, Ollama automatically generates a new SSH key pair in the volume. We retrieve the public key:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-bash hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;bash&quot;&gt;$ docker exec ollama-instance-b cat /root/.ollama/id_ed25519.pub
ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIDDQ+dvnfmuo49q5O8LOlvgZ39SKORFw47ry9k4H2jPc&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I check that this key is &lt;strong&gt;different&lt;/strong&gt; from that of the native instance:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-bash hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;bash&quot;&gt;# Instance native
$ cat /usr/share/ollama/.ollama/id_ed25519.pub
ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIGyF...(différente)

# Instance Docker
$ cat ~/ollama-b-data/id_ed25519.pub
ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIDDQ...(différente)&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Two distinct keys, two separate identities. The trick is done.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;admonitionblock note&quot;&gt;
&lt;table&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;icon&quot;&gt;
&lt;i class=&quot;fa icon-note&quot; title=&quot;ℹ️&quot;&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If your container restarts, it &lt;strong&gt;reuses&lt;/strong&gt; the keys present in the volume. The identity is persistent. You will not lose the association with the Pro account.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect2&quot;&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;_step_4_save_the_key_on_ollama_com&quot;&gt;Step 4 : Save the Key on Ollama.com&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Go to &lt;a href=&quot;https://ollama.com/settings/keys&quot; class=&quot;bare&quot;&gt;https://ollama.com/settings/keys&lt;/a&gt; → Add SSH Key. Paste the public key of the Docker instance and confirm.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Then, we link this identity to the Pro B account:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-bash hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;bash&quot;&gt;$ docker exec -it ollama-instance-b ollama signin&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The browser opens, we log in with account B&amp;#8217;s email, and the token is associated with the container&amp;#8217;s SSH key. We check that everything is OK:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-bash hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;bash&quot;&gt;$ docker exec -it ollama-instance-b ollama signin
User: cherolivpro&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect2&quot;&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;_step_5_test_with_a_small_free_model&quot;&gt;Step 5: Test with a Small Free Model&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Before I throw out the price of a Pro subscription, I want to be sure the pipeline works. I pull a small local free model to validate the connectivity:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-bash hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;bash&quot;&gt;$ docker exec ollama-instance-b ollama pull qwen3:0.6b
pulling manifest
pulling 7f4030143c1c: 100% ▕██████████████████▏ 522 MB
success

$ curl -s http://localhost:11435/api/tags
{&quot;models&quot;:[{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;qwen3:0.6b&quot;,&quot;model&quot;:&quot;qwen3:0.6b&quot;,...}]}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The port`11435`It replies, the model is served. Instance B is alive.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Once reassured, I switch to the pull of the true cloud Pro model:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-bash hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;bash&quot;&gt;$ docker exec ollama-instance-b ollama pull deepseek-v4-pro:cloud
pulling manifest
pulling 31c3059e137e: 100% ▕██████████████████▏  344 B
success&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;344 bytes for the manifest of a cloud model — normal, the inference is done server-side, not locally. And here is the ultimate test:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-bash hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;bash&quot;&gt;$ curl -s http://localhost:11435/v1/chat/completions \
  -H &quot;Content-Type: application/json&quot; \
  -d &#39;{&quot;model&quot;:&quot;deepseek-v4-pro:cloud&quot;,&quot;messages&quot;:[{&quot;role&quot;:&quot;user&quot;,&quot;content&quot;:&quot;Dis bonjour en une phrase courte.&quot;}]}&#39;

{
  &quot;id&quot;: &quot;chatcmpl-480&quot;,
  &quot;model&quot;: &quot;deepseek-v4-pro&quot;,
  &quot;choices&quot;: [{
    &quot;message&quot;: { &quot;content&quot;: &quot;Bonjour !&quot; },
    &quot;finish_reason&quot;: &quot;stop&quot;
  }],
  &quot;usage&quot;: { &quot;total_tokens&quot;: 181 }
}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Hello to you too, instance B.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_the_trap_of_the_api_v1models&quot;&gt;The Trap of the API `/v1/models&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I lost 20 minutes on a stupid detail. When I configured the provider`ollama-b`In OpenCode, nothing appeared in the model selector. Nothing. Nada.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The reason? The API`/v1/models`of instance B was returning`{&quot;object&quot;:&quot;list&quot;,&quot;data&quot;:null}`instead of`{&quot;object&quot;:&quot;list&quot;,&quot;data&quot;:[]}`when no model was being pulled. The value`null`was causing the parsing to crash on the OpenCode side, which did not display the provider.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The solution: declare the models &lt;strong&gt;explicitly&lt;/strong&gt; in the OpenCode configuration rather than relying on dynamic discovery.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;title&quot;&gt;~/.config/opencode/opencode.json&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-json hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;json&quot;&gt;{
  &quot;$schema&quot;: &quot;https://opencode.ai/config.json&quot;,
  &quot;provider&quot;: {
    &quot;ollama&quot;: {
      &quot;npm&quot;: &quot;@ai-sdk/openai-compatible&quot;,
      &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Ollama (local)&quot;,
      &quot;options&quot;: { &quot;baseURL&quot;: &quot;http://localhost:11434/v1&quot; },
      &quot;models&quot;: {
        &quot;gemma4:e2b&quot;: { &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Gemma 4 E2B (local)&quot; }
      }
    },
    &quot;ollama-b&quot;: {
      &quot;npm&quot;: &quot;@ai-sdk/openai-compatible&quot;,
      &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Ollama Instance B (Docker)&quot;,
      &quot;options&quot;: { &quot;baseURL&quot;: &quot;http://localhost:11435/v1&quot; },
      &quot;models&quot;: {
        &quot;qwen3:0.6b&quot;: { &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Qwen3 0.6B (B)&quot; },
        &quot;deepseek-v4-pro:cloud&quot;: { &quot;name&quot;: &quot;DeepSeek V4 Pro (B)&quot; }
      }
    }
  }
}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With this explicit declaration, OpenCode immediately sees provider B and its models. A session restart, and the selector`/models`displays the two instances side by side.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;admonitionblock tip&quot;&gt;
&lt;table&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;icon&quot;&gt;
&lt;i class=&quot;fa icon-tip&quot; title=&quot;💡&quot;&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If you don&amp;#8217;t see your custom provider in`/models`, don&amp;#8217;t waste three hours restarting your terminal. Declare the models manually in`opencode.json`— it solves the problem instantly.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_result_two_sessions_two_accounts_zero_conflict&quot;&gt;Result: Two Sessions, Two Accounts, Zero Conflict&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In the end, I can run two OpenCode sessions simultaneously:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-text hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;text&quot;&gt;Session 1 → /models → Ollama (local) → deepseek-v4-pro:cloud → Compte A
Session 2 → /models → Ollama Instance B (Docker) → deepseek-v4-pro:cloud → Compte B&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Each session has its own quota, its own rate limit, and they don&amp;#8217;t step on each other&amp;#8217;s toes. Same machine, same credit card, two different emails.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;And the best? The Docker container is`restart: always`. It survives system reboots without manual intervention.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_lessons_learned&quot;&gt;Lessons Learned&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;olist arabic&quot;&gt;
&lt;ol class=&quot;arabic&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Docker isolates everything, even identity&lt;/strong&gt;— A simple volume bind mount is enough to give a container its own set of SSH keys, making it indistinguishable from a different physical machine to Ollama.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Two emails, same CB&lt;/strong&gt;— Ollama does not block multiple subscriptions from the same payment method. Only the email must be distinct.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Test for free before paying&lt;/strong&gt; — Un &lt;code&gt;ollama pull qwen3:0.6b&lt;/code&gt; (modèle libre, 522 Mo) permet de valider toute la chaîne réseau sans débourser un centime. Vous validez le plomberie d&amp;#8217;abord, vous activez le Pro ensuite.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;/v1/models` with &lt;code&gt;data: null&lt;/code&gt; breaks OpenCode&lt;/strong&gt;— If you set up a custom provider with`&quot;models&quot;: {}`, OpenCode attempts to discover the models via the API. If the API responds`data: null`, the provider does not appear. Declare the models explicitly.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Two accounts, that&amp;#8217;s human multi-processing&lt;/strong&gt;— One Pro account = an active OpenCode session. Two accounts = two parallel sessions. For someone juggling multiple Gradle projects simultaneously, that’s a game changer.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_to_go_further&quot;&gt;To go further&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;ulist&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://ollama.com/settings/keys&quot;&gt;Ollama Settings — SSH Key Management&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://docs.ollama.com/&quot;&gt;Official Ollama documentation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://hub.docker.com/r/ollama/ollama&quot;&gt;Docker Ollama image on Docker Hub&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://opencode.ai/docs/providers/#ollama&quot;&gt;OpenCode — Configuration Provider Ollama&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Article published on 2026-05-08&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

