&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div id=&quot;toc&quot; class=&quot;toc&quot;&gt;
&lt;div id=&quot;toctitle&quot;&gt;Table des matières&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;ul class=&quot;sectlevel1&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#_the_accident_waiting_to_happen&quot;&gt;The Accident Waiting to Happen&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;ul class=&quot;sectlevel2&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#_the_true_question&quot;&gt;The True Question&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#_the_solution_oss_and_css&quot;&gt;The Solution: OSS/ and CSS/&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#_why_oss_and_css_and_not_public_and_private&quot;&gt;Why &quot;OSS&quot; and &quot;CSS&quot; and Not &quot;Public&quot; and &quot;Private&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;ul class=&quot;sectlevel2&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#_the_complete_tree_four_zones_not_three&quot;&gt;The Complete Tree — Four Zones, Not Three&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#_the_special_case_cheroliv_com&quot;&gt;The Special Case: cheroliv.com&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;ul class=&quot;sectlevel2&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#_bakery_gradle_doesnt_care&quot;&gt;bakery-gradle doesn&amp;#8217;t care&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#_impact_on_governance_agent&quot;&gt;Impact on Governance Agent&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#_what_we_gain_and_what_we_lose&quot;&gt;What We Gain (And What We Lose)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#_conclusion_the_physical_signal_beats_the_software_signal&quot;&gt;Conclusion: The Physical Signal Beats the Software Signal&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#_references&quot;&gt;References&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div id=&quot;preamble&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;title&quot;&gt;reading time: 10 minutes&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;foundry/&lt;/code&gt;— the functional area that hosts the code of my workspace — contains 18 projects. Eight are open source under Apache 2.0. One is closed source — the SaaS Edster. For months, these nine projects coexisted. in the same folder, separated only by&amp;#8230;&amp;#8203; nothing. Their public/private status It was information in my head, not in the file system.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;And then I wanted to hook up a RAG. And then everything fell apart.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_the_accident_waiting_to_happen&quot;&gt;The Accident Waiting to Happen&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In April 2026, I started implementing the pgvector RAG for slider-gradle. The principle: indexing all repositories of`foundry/&lt;code&gt;, producing some embeddings, and inject them into the LLM context so that it has a perception&quot; of the file`foundry/&lt;/code&gt;}.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The pipeline was simple:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-kotlin hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;kotlin&quot;&gt;val repos = fileTree(rootDir) {
    include(&quot;**/*.adoc&quot;, &quot;**/*.kts&quot;, &quot;**/*.kt&quot;, &quot;**/*.json&quot;, &quot;**/*.yml&quot;)
}
val chunks = repos.map { chunk(it) }
val embeddings = chunks.map { embed(it) }
pgvector.insert(embeddings)&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Simple. Effective. And&lt;strong&gt;dangerous&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Because this`fileTree`does not distinguish between`plantuml-gradle/` (Apache 2.0, public) and`edster/`(closed source, private). He eats everything. And if one day I publish these embeddings — on a dashboard, in a response LLM, in a training dataset — Edster&amp;#8217;s proprietary code is leaking.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;admonitionblock caution&quot;&gt;
&lt;table&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;icon&quot;&gt;
&lt;i class=&quot;fa icon-caution&quot; title=&quot;🔒&quot;&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The problem isn&amp;#8217;t that an LLM reads closed-source code. The problem, It&amp;#8217;s that this code ends up in a public vector embedding — irreversible, non-deletable, non-auditable&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect2&quot;&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;_the_true_question&quot;&gt;The True Question&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It is not &quot;how to prevent the LLM from leaking code?&quot;. It is &quot;how make escape structurally impossible?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The answer is not a prompt. The answer is to cut the folder in half.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_the_solution_oss_and_css&quot;&gt;The Solution: OSS/ and CSS/&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Before:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-none hljs&quot;&gt;foundry/
├── plantuml-gradle/       ← public
├── bakery-gradle/         ← public
├── magic-stick/           ← public
├── edster/                ← PRIVÉ
├── slider-gradle/         ← public
└── ...                    ← mélange invisible&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;After:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-none hljs&quot;&gt;foundry/
├── OSS/                   ← tout est Apache 2.0
│   ├── plantuml-gradle/
│   ├── bakery-gradle/
│   ├── magic-stick/
│   ├── slider-gradle/
│   └── ...
└── CSS/                   ← tout est closed source / private
    └── edster/&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Le `fileTree`becomes :&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-kotlin hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;kotlin&quot;&gt;val ossRepos = fileTree(File(rootDir, &quot;OSS&quot;)) {
    include(&quot;**/*.adoc&quot;, &quot;**/*.kts&quot;, &quot;**/*.kt&quot;, &quot;**/*.json&quot;, &quot;**/*.yml&quot;)
}
val cssRepos = fileTree(File(rootDir, &quot;CSS&quot;)) {
    include(&quot;**/*.adoc&quot;, &quot;**/*.kts&quot;, &quot;**/*.kt&quot;, &quot;**/*.json&quot;, &quot;**/*.yml&quot;)
}

// Embeddings publics — OSS seulement
pgvectorPublic.insert(ossRepos.map { chunk(it) }.map { embed(it) })

// Dataset fine-tuning privé — CSS seulement
fineTuningDataset.insert(cssRepos.map { chunk(it) })  // jamais publié&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;admonitionblock important&quot;&gt;
&lt;table&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;icon&quot;&gt;
&lt;i class=&quot;fa icon-important&quot; title=&quot;❗&quot;&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Public RAG never sees`CSS/`. The closed-source code feeds a dataset deprived of fine-tuning — an internal model that never leaves my home. The The separation is mechanical, not declarative.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_why_oss_and_css_and_not_public_and_private&quot;&gt;Why &quot;OSS&quot; and &quot;CSS&quot; and Not &quot;Public&quot; and &quot;Private&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The choice of the acronyms OSS (Open Source Software) and CSS (Closed Source Software) it is resolved:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;ulist&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Public&quot;/&quot;Private&quot; describes the&lt;strong&gt;visibility&lt;/strong&gt;(what GitHub sees)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;OSS&quot;/&quot;CSS&quot; describes the&lt;strong&gt;nature&lt;/strong&gt;code (what the LLM must know)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;GitHub visibility is metadata for the remote repository. The nature of the code is a property of the content. The LLM does not have access to the GitHub API — but it has access to the file system.&lt;code&gt;CSS/`he said to him, &quot;Attention, this code is not under license free&quot; without needing to read a`LICENSE`or to parse a`package.json&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;admonitionblock tip&quot;&gt;
&lt;table&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;icon&quot;&gt;
&lt;i class=&quot;fa icon-tip&quot; title=&quot;💡&quot;&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The folder name is the most robust metadata you can give to an LLM. It cannot be mis-parsed, ignored, or misinterpreted. The LLM sees`CSS/edster/`in the file path — it knows.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect2&quot;&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;_the_complete_tree_four_zones_not_three&quot;&gt;The Complete Tree — Four Zones, Not Three&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The OSS/CSS granularization complements the three-zone ontology. The zone `foundry/`goes from 1 to 2 functional sub-zones:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div id=&quot;diag-four-zone-workspace&quot; class=&quot;imageblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;img src=&quot;./images/diag-plantuml-md5-b2aba53505ebdf7e303e04717de762e4.svg&quot; alt=&quot;L&amp;#8217;organisation de foundry/ en deux sous-zones OSS et CSS&quot; width=&quot;1783&quot; height=&quot;5736&quot;&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;| Zone | GDPR | Public RAG indexing | Dataset fine-tuning | | Root | Level 0 — Intimate | ✗ | ✗ | |&lt;code&gt;configuration/&lt;/code&gt;| Level 1 — Restricted | ✗ | ✗ | (empty)&lt;code&gt;office/&lt;/code&gt;| Level 2 — Collaborative | ✓ Filtered | ✓ Filtered | | &lt;code&gt;foundry/private/&lt;/code&gt;| Level 3 — Conditional open | ✗ | ✓ Private | |&lt;code&gt;foundry/public/&lt;/code&gt;| Level 4 — Native audience | ✓ Free | ✓ Public |&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_the_special_case_cheroliv_com&quot;&gt;The Special Case: cheroliv.com&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;While I was at it, I fixed another inconsistency. My site &lt;code&gt;cheroliv.com`lived in`foundry/`as a software project fully-fledged — with its own Gradle build, its own CI, its own governance&lt;/code&gt;.agents/`.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;But blog posts are not code. They are &lt;strong&gt;data&lt;/strong&gt; editorials** of Cercle 2 — on an equal footing with the framings of`office/pilotage/` or the training of`office/formations/`.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-none hljs&quot;&gt;AVANT                            APRÈS
foundry/                office/
  cheroliv.com/                    sites/
    site/jbake/content/blog/         cheroliv.com/
      2026/0117_....adoc               2026/0117_....adoc&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;What changes:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;ulist&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The articles are in`office/`→ confidentiality of Circle 2&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The task`publishSite`becomes a capability of`engine`&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;via`bakery-gradle`— the plugin receives a`FileTree`, he does not know that the articles come from`office/`&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;ulist&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;One CI, one governance — zero duplication&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The Vision/Opinion classifier (Rule 2bis) applies automatically:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;the articles in`office/`are filtered before publishing&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect2&quot;&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;_bakery_gradle_doesnt_care&quot;&gt;bakery-gradle doesn&amp;#8217;t care&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;And that&amp;#8217;s what&amp;#8217;s beautiful. The plugin`bakery-gradle`has not been modified. It receives an AsciiDoc content directory, it generates HTML, it pushes on GitHub Pages. That this directory is called`site/jbake/content/` ou &lt;code&gt;office/sites/cheroliv/&lt;/code&gt;— the plugin doesn&amp;#8217;t care.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-kotlin hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;kotlin&quot;&gt;// engine/build.gradle.kts
task(&quot;publishBlog&quot;) {
    doLast {
        val articles = fileTree(&quot;../../office/sites/cheroliv/2026/&quot;)
        bakery.generate(articles)  // ← bakery ne sait pas d&#39;où ça vient
    }
}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The interface contract is a Gradle task. Not a REST API. Not a webhook. A task — typed, testable, executable locally and in CI.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_impact_on_governance_agent&quot;&gt;Impact on Governance Agent&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The OSS/CSS granularization modifies the agent governance on three points:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;olist arabic&quot;&gt;
&lt;ol class=&quot;arabic&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;RAG public&lt;/strong&gt; : the indexing scope changes from`foundry/**`&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;à &lt;code&gt;foundry/public/&lt;strong&gt;&lt;/code&gt; + &lt;code&gt;office/Vision/&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/code&gt;. The code closed source is excluded from the index by task configuration, not by prompt.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;olist arabic&quot;&gt;
&lt;ol class=&quot;arabic&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Knowledge Graph&lt;/strong&gt; : graphify-gradle produces two graphs :&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;*&lt;code&gt;office/graph.json&lt;/code&gt;(public) — relations between OSS artifacts + office/Vision *&lt;code&gt;configuration/graph_private.json&lt;/code&gt;(gitignored, private) — relations between CSS artifacts, never published&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;olist arabic&quot;&gt;
&lt;ol class=&quot;arabic&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Dataset fine-tuning&lt;/strong&gt;:`codebase-gradle`now has two sources :&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;*&lt;code&gt;OSS/&lt;/code&gt;→ public datasets (community models, benchmarks) *&lt;code&gt;CSS/&lt;/code&gt;→ private datasets (internal model, proprietary fine‑tuning)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_what_we_gain_and_what_we_lose&quot;&gt;What We Gain (And What We Lose)&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;table class=&quot;tableblock frame-all grid-all stretch&quot;&gt;
&lt;colgroup&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 7.1428%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 7.1428%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 7.1428%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 7.1428%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 7.1428%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 7.1428%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 7.1428%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 7.1428%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 7.1428%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 7.1428%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 7.1428%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 7.1428%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 7.1428%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 7.1436%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;/colgroup&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;Front (flat file)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;After (OSS/CSS + office/sites)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;&lt;code&gt;ls foundry/&lt;/code&gt;→ public/private mix&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;&lt;code&gt;ls foundry/public/&lt;/code&gt;→ everything is publishable&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;RAG indexes everything without discrimination&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;RAG indexes`OSS/`+`office/Vision`exclusively&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;Impossible to know if a project is open source&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;The path`OSS/` ou `CSS/`the said&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;cheroliv.com has its own CI, its own governance&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;CI and governance are pooled in engine&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;Blog posts are in a &#39;code&#39; repository&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;Blog posts are in`office/sites/`— data, not code&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;Risk of leaking closed-source code in public embeddings&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;Structurally impossible —`CSS/`out of scope RAG&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The only cost: a`git mv`overall across the 17 OSS repositories + a refactor of the &lt;code&gt;build.gradle.kts&lt;/code&gt; de &lt;code&gt;engine&lt;/code&gt;. This is a cold migration — no data in flight, no users impacted.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_conclusion_the_physical_signal_beats_the_software_signal&quot;&gt;Conclusion: The Physical Signal Beats the Software Signal&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;What I did this afternoon was replace an invisible convention by a visible separation in the area`foundry/&lt;code&gt;. Before, you had to &lt;strong&gt;to know&lt;/strong&gt; that`edster/`was closed source. Now you &lt;strong&gt;see&lt;/strong&gt; it — the folder is called`CSS/&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It&amp;#8217;s the same principle as Unix permissions, network VLANs, or the Fire compartments in a database. Safety must not be information that you must remember. It must be a property &lt;strong&gt;physics&lt;/strong&gt;of the system.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;OSS/CSS granularization is the translation of this principle into the field of LLM governance. The LLM does not need to know that Edster is confidential. It needs to be unable to index it.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;And that&amp;#8217;s exactly what`CSS/`guarantees.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_references&quot;&gt;References&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;ulist&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;0118_matrice_gouvernance_llm_zone_acces_workspace_post.html&quot;&gt;Article 0117 — The LLM Governance Matrix&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;0114_gouvernance_cercles_confiance_ontologie_spatiale_alignement_llm_post.html&quot;&gt;Article 0114 — The Trust Circles&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;0116_compartimentage_epistemique_automatise_llm_post.html&quot;&gt;Article 0116 — Automated Epistemic Compartmentalization&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;0108_gouvernance_agent_opencode_eager_lazy_post.html&quot;&gt;Article 0108 — Governing an AI Agent with AsciiDoc&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div id=&quot;toc&quot; class=&quot;toc&quot;&gt;
&lt;div id=&quot;toctitle&quot;&gt;Table des matières&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;ul class=&quot;sectlevel1&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#_the_problem_thinking_freely_without_publishing_anything&quot;&gt;The Problem: Thinking Freely Without Publishing Anything&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#_the_stimulus_pattern_be_born_fix_dilute_die&quot;&gt;The STIMULUS Pattern — Be born, Fix, Dilute, Die&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#_the_delegation_to_the_llm_why_its_the_llm_that_filters&quot;&gt;The Delegation to the LLM: Why It&amp;#8217;s the LLM That Filters&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div id=&quot;preamble&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;title&quot;&gt;reading time: 10 minutes&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;My LLM does not merely execute my Gradle tasks. It is the guardian of the border between my private thought and my public expression. Each idea is classified — Vision, Opinion, Strategy — before being routed. Here is how this automated epistemic compartmentalization protects my mental garden without silencing me.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;dlist&quot;&gt;
&lt;dl&gt;
&lt;dt class=&quot;hdlist1&quot;&gt;tic&lt;/dt&gt;
&lt;dd&gt;
&lt;p&gt;[]&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/dd&gt;
&lt;/dl&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_the_problem_thinking_freely_without_publishing_anything&quot;&gt;The Problem: Thinking Freely Without Publishing Anything&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;When a solo developer documents his thought process with an LLM, he encounters a paradox:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-none hljs&quot;&gt;CAPTURE TOUT (Cercle 0, jardin secret)
  ├→ Brain dump libre, pas de censure
  ├→ Idées brutes, spéculations, intuitions non vérifiées
  ├→ Allers-retours LLM : challenger, itérer, raffiner
  └→ Le LLM voit TOUT — le bon, le flou, l&#39;embryonnaire

PUBLICATION SÉLECTIVE (Cercle 4, blog public)
  ├→ Uniquement les décisions actées
  ├→ Uniquement les patterns testés (&amp;gt;100 tests PASS)
  ├→ Uniquement ce qui a de la valeur pour le lecteur
  └→ JAMAIS une spéculation présentée comme une vérité&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Between these two extremes, there is a&lt;strong&gt;filter&lt;/strong&gt;. The question is: who does it? A tired human at the end of a session? A naive regex script? Or the LLM itself, which has already read everything?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;My answer: the LLM.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_the_stimulus_pattern_be_born_fix_dilute_die&quot;&gt;The STIMULUS Pattern — Be born, Fix, Dilute, Die&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The STIMULUS is a file`.adoc`temporary at the root of the workspace (Circle 0). Its name follows an evocative convention —&lt;code&gt;PICTURE_ME_ROLLIN.adoc&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;HOLD_MY_BEER.adoc&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;ONCE_UPON_A_TIME.adoc&lt;/code&gt;— which captures the exploratory intention of the moment.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Its life cycle is merciless :&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-none hljs&quot;&gt;CRÉATION (Cercle 0)
  ├→ Fichier vide, brain dump libre
  ├→ Allers-retours LLM ← → développeur
  └→ Aucune pression de structure ou de publication

MATURATION
  ├→ Le contenu se densifie
  ├→ Des sections émergent naturellement
  └→ Le fichier devient une référence informelle de session

CLASSIFICATION ÉPISTÉMIQUE ← FAIT PAR LE LLM
  ├→ VISION    → publiable, diluer dans les docs publics + blog
  ├→ OPINION   → confiner au Cercle 0, pas de dilution externe
  └→ STRATÉGIE → dilution restreinte (docs racine, PAS de blog)

DILUTION (Cascade vers Cercles légitimes)
  ├→ VISION → WORKSPACE_AS_PRODUCT.adoc + WHAT_THE_GAMES.adoc + blog
  ├→ STRATÉGIE → WORKSPACE_AS_PRODUCT.adoc (section business)
  └→ OPINION → reste dans le stimulus, ne bouge pas

ARCHIVAGE + SUPPRESSION
  ├→ Snapshot dans configuration/vision-archive/ (trace historique)
  └→ rm du stimulus — il a fini son travail&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A stimulus that survives more than 2 sessions without dilution is an anti-pattern. Either the information is too important to rot in a temporary file (→ dilute), or it is too vague to be worth existing (→ delete).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_the_delegation_to_the_llm_why_its_the_llm_that_filters&quot;&gt;The Delegation to the LLM: Why It&amp;#8217;s the LLM That Filters&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I do not hardcode the Vision/Opinion classification in Kotlin. I inject it into the&lt;strong&gt;prompt system&lt;/strong&gt;of the LLM via`AGENT_GOVERNANCE.adoc`:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-none hljs&quot;&gt;----
----
Pour chaque dilution de stimulus ou session, tu es responsable de classifier
le contenu :
- VISION : décision architecturale actée, pattern testé en production (&amp;gt;100 tests PASS),
  décision documentée dans BACKLOG ou ROADMAP
- OPINION : spéculation, hypothèse non validée par des tests, intuition
  sans ancrage expérimental
- STRATEGIE : positionnement business, pricing, marché cible

Règles :
- Si OPINION, le contenu reste confiné dans son cercle d&#39;origine
- Si VISION, tu procèdes à la dilution dans les documents racine
- Si STRATEGIE, dilution restreinte (docs racine, pas de blog public)
- Cette classification détermine aussi si un article de blog est publiable
  sur cheroliv.com
----

The LLM reads this directive**before**to process the session. It is in its immediate context, not in a separate file. It&#39;s *deterministic prompt engineering*: the behavior emerges from the injected text, not from an external classifier.

=== Why It Works — and Why It&#39;s Counterintuitive

The standard reaction: &quot;You entrust the classification to the AI? And if it gets it wrong?

Answer: humans don&#39;t do any better at 2 a.m. The difference is that the LLM, on the other hand, reads everything. It has the full context of the session and the target documents. It knows that`VPS ECO-16 12€ HT`is an infrastructure decision (VISION) while`« le TTS ElevenLabs sonne plus naturel que Coqui »`is an opinion not verified by a benchmark.

The human, at the end of the session, skims and does their best. The LLM reads exhaustively and applies a systematic grid. The result is**more reliable**, no less.

And for the 20% of ambiguous cases — the gray area where even the LLM hesitates — the answer is not &#39;add code&#39;. The answer is**LangGraph4j**(EPIC 13): a state graph that, for ambiguous cases, activates a Human-in-the-Loop node and escalates to the human for decision. But for the 80% of clear cases, the LLM suffices.

== The Complete Pipe: from Brain Dump to Blog Post

Here is the complete flow — from the raw idea to the published article — with the LLM as the sole filter:

[source]
----
CERCLE 0 (jardin secret)
  PICTURE_ME_ROLLIN.adoc   ← brain dump, allers-retours LLM
       ↓
  [LLM classifie] → VISION / OPINION / STRATÉGIE
       ↓
  VISION → dilution dans WORKSPACE_AS_PRODUCT.adoc
         → dilution dans WHAT_THE_GAMES_BEEN_MISSING.adoc
         → dilution dans WORKSPACE_VISION.adoc (session context)
         → RÉDACTION ARTICLE BLOG ← le LLM écrit, pas le dev
       ↓
CERCLE 4 (blog public)
  0116_compartimentage_epistemique.adoc  ← publié sur cheroliv.com
       ↓
  ./gradlew deploy (bakery-gradle → JBake → JGit → GitHub Pages)
       ↓
  https://cheroliv.com/blog/2026/0116/  ← live
----

The developer does not write the article. He challenges, reviews, validates. The LLM writes. The loop is:

1. Free brain dump → no censorship
2. Epistemic classification by the LLM → no leak
3. Writing by the LLM → no procrastination
4. Human review → no error

The historical bottleneck — &quot;I don&#39;t have time to write&quot; — is eliminated. The LLM does the heavy lifting. The human does what only a human can do: judge.

== The Three Root Documents — How Information Finds Its Place

Each target document has a precise role in the dilution cascade:

[cols=&quot;1,3,2&quot;]
|===
|Document |What he receives |Content type |`WORKSPACE_AS_PRODUCT.adoc` |Architectural decisions, technical stack, roadmap, business model |VISION + STRATEGY |`WHAT_THE_GAMES_BEEN_MISSING.adoc` |Patterns, lessons, anti-patterns, ontology, formal rules |VISION (tested patterns) |`WORKSPACE_VISION.adoc` |Session context, decision history, infrastructure points |VISION + STRATEGY (context) |`AGENT_GOVERNANCE.adoc` |Governance rules, project portfolio, procedures |VISION (governance) |**Blog cheroliv.com** |Public articles, value for the external reader |VISION only
|===

The same stimulus can feed the four documents + the blog — each destination receives the portion that pertains to it, without redundancy, without contradiction.

This is what happened for`PICTURE_ME_ROLLIN.adoc`(3 May 2026) :

| Information | Classification | Destinations | | Inventory of 13+ foundry projects | VISION | WORKSPACE_AS_PRODUCT | | VPS-2 OVH, edster.cloud, 8.49€/month | VISION | WORKSPACE_AS_PRODUCT + WORKSPACE_VISION | The African market is ready for mobile money | « DeepSeek &amp;gt; Kimi &amp;gt; GLM for long context » | OPINION → VISION | Confined then promoted after benchmark (article 0112) | | Synthetic Employee as final product | STRATEGY | WORKSPACE_AS_PRODUCT (no blog) |

== The Baby-Step Pattern + TestContainer + Living Doc — the Code that Tests Itself

The STIMULUS/Dilution pattern manages thought. Its equivalent for code is the Baby-Step/TestContainer/Living Documentation pattern, validated by plantuml-gradle (380/380 tests PASS, 137 sessions without Docker leak):

[cols=&quot;1,3&quot;]
|===
|Principle |Rule |**Baby-step** |Never more than 3 points per US. If a US is scary, split it. A 1-point US that passes &amp;gt; an 8-point US that drags. |**TestContainer first** |Before the business code, the container. Infra test passes → stable environment guaranteed. |**Dataset committed** |Minimal but representative subset in`src/test/resources/`, without PII. Clonable, launchable. |**Living documentation** |Each Cucumber test = a sentence in natural language. The source code is the executable spec. Zero Markdown docs to maintain. |**Automatic cleanup** |Zero resource leak. Orphaned containers,`/tmp/gradle-test-*`: everything is cleaned.
|===

This pattern is the reason why plantuml-gradle has never had a Docker regression in 138 sessions. It guarantees that the code that talks to an external infra (PostgreSQL, Redis, Kafka) is always tested in an environment identical to production — versioned, reproducible, launchable in one command.

== Conclusion: Freedom of Thought without the Fear of Publishing

Automated epistemic compartmentalization is not a censorship tool. It&#39;s a**liberator**. It allows thinking freely, speculating without filter, brain-dumping everything that comes to mind — never fearing that an embryonic idea ends up on the blog.

The LLM is the guardian of this frontier. It reads everything. It classifies. It dilutes what deserves to survive. It confines what must remain private. It drafts what is publishable.

And the developer, for his part, has the final say: review, challenge, validate. Judge.

It&#39;s the division of labor that was missing from the solo developer: a synthetic employee handling mental paperwork, so the human can focus on what they do best — design, architect, decide.

