&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_what_this_migration_says_about_dogfooding&quot;&gt;What this migration says about dogfooding&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This site is generated by my own Gradle plugin`bakery`. The contact form lives inside the site. The Supabase → Firebase migration is documented in`AGENT.adoc`, it is discussed in the backlog, it is tested via`./gradlew serve`, and it generates a blog post (the one you are reading).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It&amp;#8217;s pure dogfooding. The site is the product of the plugin, the plugin is the product of the developer, the developer documents the process on the site itself.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The loop is closed.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Having kept a mock around for months (entire sessions where the form silently lied) made me realize something: the backlog of a personal static site is never &#39;finished&#39;. There&amp;#8217;s always a prioritized user story, always a draft article, always a commented section in a template. Discipline isn&amp;#8217;t about finishing everything — it&amp;#8217;s about finishing what&amp;#8217;s visible to the user.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A broken contact form is worse than having no contact form at all. It&amp;#8217;s an unfulfilled promise.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
