&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Each session started with about 30K tokens of EAGER context:`AGENT.adoc`(287 lines)&lt;code&gt;PROMPT_REPRISE.adoc&lt;/code&gt;(51 lines),&lt;code&gt;.agents/INDEX.adoc&lt;/code&gt;(218 lines),&lt;code&gt;LAZY_EAGER_ESSENTIALS.adoc&lt;/code&gt;(50 lines). The context grew quickly with the exchanges — a typical session of 10 messages added 15-20K tokens to the cumulative prompt.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_the_evaluation_grid&quot;&gt;The Evaluation Grid&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I evaluated each model on four critical axes for assisted software development:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;table class=&quot;tableblock frame-all grid-all stretch&quot;&gt;
&lt;colgroup&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 10%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 10%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 10%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 10%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 10%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 10%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 10%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 10%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 10%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 10%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;/colgroup&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;Axis&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;Concrete criterion&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Long context coherence&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;Does the agent remember the conventions decided 40 messages ago?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Quality of the produced code&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;Does the code compile on the first try? Does it respect existing patterns?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Architectural reasoning&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;Does the agent understand the relationships between modules without me having to re‑explain them?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Resistance to hallucinations&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;tableblock halign-left valign-top&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;tableblock&quot;&gt;From how many tokens does the agent start to invent nonexistent APIs or classes?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;And a home-made synthetic metric: the&lt;strong&gt;recovery coefficient&lt;/strong&gt;(how much time I spend correcting the agent instead of coding with him).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_round_1_kimi_k2_6_the_false_start&quot;&gt;Round 1 : Kimi K2.6 — The False Start&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Kimi K2.6 was my first choice. Its benchmarks on SWE-Bench Verified (80.2) and Terminal-Bench 2.0 (66.7) are excellent. Its MLA architecture promises good efficiency on long sequences.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect2&quot;&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;_session_9_the_improvement&quot;&gt;Session 9: the improvement&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;First session with Kimi. Task: implement the method`resolveActiveKey()&lt;code&gt;in`codebase.kt&lt;/code&gt;— an API key resolution function with CLI fallback. The context is 30K tokens, Kimi reasons quickly, produces clean code with key expiration handling.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-kotlin hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;kotlin&quot;&gt;fun resolveActiveKey(
    cfg: CodebaseConfiguration,
    logger: Logger,
    cliProvider: String? = null,
    cliAccount: String? = null,
    cliKey: String? = null
): NamedApiKey? {
    // Résolution provider → compte → clé avec CLI override
    // Kimi a parfaitement compris la chaîne de priorité
}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The code compiles. The 7 test cases pass. I am optimistic.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect2&quot;&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;_session_10_the_silent_shipwreck&quot;&gt;Session 10 : the silent shipwreck&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Second session. The context climbs to ~90K tokens with the exchanges. I ask Kimi to add an AsciiDoc snapshot mechanism that anonymizes secrets before writing.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;That&amp;#8217;s where it derails. Kimi starts to invent classes that don&amp;#8217;t exist. He offers me`AnonymizedObjectMapper`— a fictitious class. He confuses`ReadmeYmlAnonymizer` et &lt;code&gt;CodebaseYmlAnonymizer&lt;/code&gt;. He suggests that I import`com.fasterxml.jackson.anonymize.*`— a package that never existed.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Worse: in his thinking phase, I see that he is building arguments on false premises. He &quot;remembers&quot; that`GitConfig`has a field`anonymizedToken`— no, it&amp;#8217;s`resolvedToken()&lt;code&gt;. It attributes to`SiteYmlAnonymizer`a method`maskSupabaseCredentials()&lt;/code&gt;— which does not exist.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;__&lt;/em&gt; It wasn&amp;#8217;t a bug. It was a gradual dissolution of coherence. As if each token added to the context diluted a little more the memory of the first 30,000. &amp;lt;/think&amp;gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I stop Kimi after two sessions. The diagnosis is clear: MLA compresses the KV cache well, but without a fine-grained sparse selection mechanism, attention dilutes mechanically beyond 60K tokens. Each token &quot;sees&quot; the distant context less and less well — and starts filling the gaps with noise.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_round_2_glm_5_1_the_honorable_fighter&quot;&gt;Round 2: GLM-5.1 — The Honorable Fighter&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;GLM-5.1 arrives with a different architecture: MLA for the base model, then a &lt;strong&gt;continued pre-training&lt;/strong&gt; with DSA (DeepSeek Sparse Attention) — a lightweight indexer that dynamically selects the top-2048 relevant tokens from the entire history.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect2&quot;&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;_architecture_the_dsa_against_the_pure_mla&quot;&gt;Architecture: the DSA against the pure MLA&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The difference is fundamental. Where Kimi compresses history into a single latent space (and gradually loses the ability to discriminate relevant information), GLM grafts a post‑training indexer that makes an explicit sparse selection.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div id=&quot;diag-mla-vs-dsa&quot; class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;Failed to generate image: PlantUML preprocessing failed: [From &amp;lt;input&amp;gt; (line 9) ]

