&lt;p&gt;The timeline above illustrates the real progression. The tipping point is session 87: that&amp;#8217;s where the frustration of repeated recontextualization exceeds the tolerance threshold, and the Eager/Lazy method ceases to be an idea and becomes an obligation.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_the_strategy_eagerlazy_in_depth&quot;&gt;The Strategy: Eager/Lazy in Depth&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect2&quot;&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;_philosophy_computer_cache_applied_to_cognition&quot;&gt;Philosophy : Computer Cache Applied to Cognition&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;My approach is directly inspired by computer cache management. Everything that is&lt;strong&gt;critical and frequently used&lt;/strong&gt;must be immediately accessible&lt;strong&gt;Eager&lt;/strong&gt;). Everything that is&lt;strong&gt;contextual or bulky&lt;/strong&gt;must be loaded on demand&lt;strong&gt;Lazy&lt;/strong&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;table class=&quot;tableblock frame-all grid-all stretch&quot;&gt;
&lt;colgroup&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 6.6666%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 6.6666%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 6.6666%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 6.6666%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 6.6666%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 6.6666%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 6.6666%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 6.6666%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 6.6666%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 6.6666%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 6.6666%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 6.6666%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 6.6666%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 6.6666%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;col style=&quot;width: 6.6676%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;/colgroup&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
