The tree structure above shows the real structure of the folder`.agents/`on`plantuml-plugin`, the most mature project. Note the depth in three layers: root files (metadata), folder`sessions/`(chronological archives), and file`archives/`(aggregations and summaries). It is this depth that transforms governance from a simple TODO file into a**complete organizational memory**.

**.agents/sessions/{N}-{title}.adoc**-- The detailed archives of each session. Currently:

* `plantuml-plugin`:**133 archived sessions**(from session 1 to 133)
* `bakery-plugin`:**11 sessions**
* `magic-stick` : **23 sessions**
* `cheroliv.com` : **9 formal sessions**+ 7 pre-system sessions reconstructed retroactively

Each archive contains the full context of the session, the decisions made, the problems encountered and their resolution, the executed commands and their output.

**.agents/SESSIONS_HISTORY.adoc**-- A summary table of all sessions with a score. Example on`cheroliv.com`:

----

| -6 | 2025-05 | chore | Initialisation projet Gradle/JBake | 7/10 |  1 | 2026-04-25 | chore | Migration gouvernance agent | 8/10 |  7 | 2026-04-27 | debug/fix | Correction publishSite | 9/10 |  8 | 2026-04-27 | analyse | Analyse article 0108 | 7/10

**.agents/COMPLETED_TASKS_ARCHIVE_{mois}.adoc**-- Completed tasks archived by month, to avoid overloading the active backlog. When a user story is finished, it moves here. The backlog remains readable: maximum 10 active items.

**.agents/PROCEDURES.adoc**-- Detailed templates of the end-of-session procedure. Long, but read only once by the agent when learning the method. Then the procedure becomes mechanical.

**.agents/AGENT_MODUS_OPERANDI.adoc**-- The complete strategic documentation. On`plantuml-plugin`, this file does**900+ lines**and is actually called`AGENT_METHODOLOGIES.adoc`— I changed the name between writing this article and its effective implementation. This kind of naming divergence is inevitable in an evolving handmade system. The important thing is the naming convention: if the file documents the *method*, it starts with`AGENT_`or an explicit prefix. It documents the Eager/Lazy methodology, the patterns to follow, the anti-patterns to avoid. It is LAZY because an agent does not need to reread the entire strategy in each session, only when there is ambiguity.

**`*_REFERENCE.adoc`** -- Project specific technical references. Sur`magic-stick`, two dense LAZY files:

* `AB_PARTITION_REFERENCE.adoc`(147 lines) -- GPT A/B partition architecture, scripts`update-system.sh`, estimated sizes, rollback mechanism
* `BOOT_TEST_REFERENCE.adoc`(144 lines) -- QEMU + VNC procedure to test boot of the ISO without physical hardware, BIOS/UEFI checklist, CI/CD limitations

On`plantuml-plugin`(Empty output)

* `ARCHITECTURE.adoc`(134 lines) -- Structure of the 11 data classes, points of attention (pitfalls to avoid), optimized test commands
* `API_KEY_POOL_REFERENCE.adoc`-- Full details of the key pool (LAZY while`ESSENTIALS`is EAGER)

== Specialized Agents: A Virtual Team

Governance is not limited to passive files. I have formalized**roles of specialized agents**in dedicated LAZY files, which define the expected workflow according to the task type.

|===
|Agent |File |Role |Project |**to code** |`CODER.adoc` |Implementation FTL/CSS/JS, semantic tags, accessibility criteria |cheroliv.com |**Scrum Master** |`SCRUM_MASTER.adoc` |US planning, sub-task breakdown, dependency detection |cheroliv.com |**PlantUML Designer** |`PLANTUML_DESIGNER.adoc` |Diagram creation, PUML syntax, JBake integration |cheroliv.com
|===

The file`CODER.adoc`on`cheroliv.com`contains concrete rules: _one`&amp;lt;h1&amp;gt;`per page_, _Prefix paths with`${content.rootpath}`_, _Declare language`&amp;lt;html lang=&quot;${content.lang!&quot;fr&quot;}&quot;&amp;gt;`_. These conventions, written once, are automatically respected by the agent since session 1.

The file`SCRUM_MASTER.adoc`imposes a deliverable structure :**Objective**, **Tasks**(coordinates with assignment),**Acceptance criteria**, **Risks**. When I ask for an action plan, the agent produces this structure without me having asked for it. The governance**program**the agent.

==== When Specialized Agents Become Indispensable

The creation of specialized agents follows a natural curve. At the beginning of a project, you don&#39;t need them —`AGENT.adoc`is more than enough. But when the project grows (let&#39;s say, beyond 20 sessions), two signals must alert you :

1. The agent mixes the conventions of two distinct fields (e.g., PlantUML syntax and CSS rules)
2. You spend more time correcting the agent on conventions that you have already explained to him 5 times.

On`cheroliv.com`, it happened during the session... 1. Yes, right from the start. Because this project is a website with three languages (FTL, CSS, JS), AsciiDoc content, and PlantUML diagrams — three domains that have nothing to do with each other. The CODER agent needs to know font sizes and media queries; the PLANTUML_DESIGNER agent needs to know the`@startuml`. Without separation, the CODER agent was proposing diagrams to me, and vice versa. The chaos.

On`jhipster-gradle-plugins`, I created two specialized agents adapted for Gradle plugin development:`PLUGIN_DEVELOPER.adoc` et `BACKLOG_MANAGER.adoc`. The first encodes all Kotlin/Gradle conventions (no`!!`python import sys

def main(): data = sys.stdin.read() parts = data.split(&#39;`&#39;) for i in range(len(parts)): if i % 2 == 0:  # outside backticks parts[i] = parts[i].replace(&quot;pour&quot;, &quot;for&quot;).replace(&quot;les&quot;, &quot;the&quot;).replace(&quot;modèles&quot;, &quot;models&quot;) sys.stdout.write(&#39;`&#39;.join(parts))

if __name__ == &#39;__main__&#39;: main()`@TaskAction`for the tasks). The second knows that`persistence`must be stable before`assistant`doesn&#39;t start its development — a critical dependency in a monorepo.

The mistake not to make: create too many agents too early.`plantuml-plugin`It waited for session 108 before formalizing a specialized agent for the API key pool. Before that, the business context was contained in`AGENT.adoc`. The rule of thumb: a specialized agent is justified when its business domain exceeds 100 lines of documentation.

==== The Session Naming Convention

A detail that seems trivial but becomes critical when you reach 100 sessions. How to name the archive files?

I learned the hard way that a convention is necessary — four projects, four different formats at the start, and I got lost. Today, the convention I have settled on is:&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;{N}-{type}-{sujet-kebab-case}.adoc&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;Concrete examples :

* `1-chore-migration-gouvernance-agent.adoc`— session 1, type chore
* `10-solidification-tests.adoc`— session 10, without explicit type (the subject suffices)
* `036-debug-graphify-symlink-epic9.adoc`— session 36 with 3-digit number for sorting

The session number is the primary sorting criterion. Projects using three‑digit numbers (001, 036, 133) avoid lexical sorting problems when exceeding 99. This is what I now use on`magic-stick`:`001-init-projet.adoc`, `036-debug-graphify-symlink-epic9.adoc`.

The type is optional and derived from the session keywords (debug, feature, refactor, docs, chore, test). The subject in kebab-case is the most important part: it must allow retrieving a session without opening the file. If you wonder « which session was it where we fixed the timeout of the integration tests? », the answer is`124-fix-timeout-integration-test.adoc`.

For reconstructed historical sessions (projects born before governance), I use negative numbers. On`cheroliv.com`, the sessions -6 to 0 cover the entire pre-governance history of the project. And for the &quot;undocumented&quot; or lost sessions, I create an entry in`SESSIONS_HISTORY.adoc`without a matching archive, with a score`?`. It&#39;s more honest than pretending.

[plantuml, format=svg, id=diag-naming-convention, alt=&quot;Arbre de décision pour le nommage des fichiers de session&quot;]&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;@startuml
skinparam backgroundColor #FEFEFE
skinparam defaultTextAlignment center
skinparam wrapWidth 200&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;title Session Naming Convention
start&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;:Une session se termine;
note right: Trigger &quot;end of session&quot;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;if (Session antérieure\nà la gouvernance ?) then (oui)
  :Numéro &lt;strong&gt;NÉGATIF&lt;/strong&gt;\n-6, -5 &amp;#8230;&amp;#8203; 0;
  note right: Historique\nreconstitué
  :Suffixe : reconstitution;
else (non)
  :Numéro &lt;strong&gt;POSITIF&lt;/strong&gt;\nsur 3 chiffres si &amp;gt; 99;
  note right: 001, 036, 133\npour le tri lexicographique&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;literalblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;:Détecter le **TYPE**;
if (Mots-clés trouvés ?) then (oui)
  :debug / feature / refactor\ndocs / chore / test;
else (non)
  :Omettre le type\n(le sujet suffit);
endif&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;literalblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;  :Formuler le **SUJET** en kebab-case;
  note right
    Ex: fix-timeout-integration-test
    Doit permettre de retrouver
    sans ouvrir le fichier
  end note
endif&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;note right
  • 1-chore-migration-gouvernance.adoc
  • 036-debug-graphify-symlink.adoc
  • 124-fix-timeout-integration-test.adoc
  • 133-epic11-article-blog-kg.adoc
end note&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;if (Session documentée ?) then (oui)
  :Créer archive dans sessions/;
  :Ajouter ligne SESSIONS_HISTORY\navec score X/10;
else (non)
  :Ajouter ligne SESSIONS_HISTORY\navec score &lt;strong&gt;?&lt;/strong&gt;\n&lt;strong&gt;sans archive&lt;/strong&gt;;
  note right: L&amp;#8217;honnêteté\nplutôt que le vide
endif&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;stop
@enduml&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;==== TEST_COVERAGE_ANALYSIS.adoc` — Step 5 Dissected

Step 5 of the session end procedure is the most mysterious. It says &#39;Update`TEST_COVERAGE_ANALYSIS.adoc`if tests have been added or modified. » But what does this file look like ?

on`plantuml-plugin`, it has evolved from a few lines to a complete structure. Here is its stabilized form:

[source]&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;h1 id=&quot;_analyse_de_couverture_de_tests&quot; class=&quot;sect0&quot;&gt;Analyse de Couverture de Tests&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_suivi_des_tests&quot;&gt;Suivi des Tests&lt;/h2&gt;
