&lt;div id=&quot;preamble&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Do you feel that your laptop is overheating abnormally under Ubuntu? This article proposes an approach&lt;strong&gt;software&lt;/strong&gt;complete for&lt;strong&gt;cool&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;monitor&lt;/strong&gt; et &lt;strong&gt;master the temperature&lt;/strong&gt;of your machine.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_objective_of_this_guide&quot;&gt;Objective of this guide&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;ulist&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Reduce the overall temperature of the computer&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Optimize energy management&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Monitor critical components (CPU, GPU, etc.)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Avoid slowdowns due to&lt;strong&gt;thermal throttling&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;imageblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;img src=&quot;gestion_thermique.svg&quot; alt=&quot;gestion thermique&quot; width=&quot;392&quot; height=&quot;298&quot;&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_prerequisite&quot;&gt;Prerequisite&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;ulist&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;An Ubuntu machine (20.04 or higher recommended)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Internet connection&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Command-line proficiency (beginner to intermediate)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_install_and_configure_the_tools&quot;&gt;Install and configure the tools&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect2&quot;&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;_lm_sensors_to_detect_the_sensors&quot;&gt;lm-sensors` to detect the sensors&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-bash hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;bash&quot;&gt;sudo apt install lm-sensors
sudo sensors-detect
sensors&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;admonitionblock note&quot;&gt;
&lt;table&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;icon&quot;&gt;
&lt;i class=&quot;fa icon-note&quot; title=&quot;Note&quot;&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Answer`YES`to all the questions of`sensors-detect`to detect the modules.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect2&quot;&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;_tlp_intelligent_energy_manager&quot;&gt;tlp` : intelligent energy manager&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-bash hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;bash&quot;&gt;sudo apt install tlp tlp-rdw
sudo systemctl enable tlp
sudo systemctl start tlp&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;imageblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;img src=&quot;tlp_etat.svg&quot; alt=&quot;tlp etat&quot; width=&quot;343&quot; height=&quot;390&quot;&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect2&quot;&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;_cpufrequtils_limit_cpu_frequencies&quot;&gt;cpufrequtils` : limit CPU frequencies&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-bash hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;bash&quot;&gt;sudo apt install cpufrequtils
cpufreq-info
sudo cpufreq-set -g powersave&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;ulist&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;powersave&lt;/code&gt;: limits frequency peaks&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;`performance`favors power at the expense of temperature&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect2&quot;&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;_auto_cpufreq_simplified_dynamic_management&quot;&gt;auto-cpufreq` : simplified dynamic management&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-bash hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;bash&quot;&gt;git clone https://github.com/AdnanHodzic/auto-cpufreq.git
cd auto-cpufreq
sudo ./auto-cpufreq-installer
sudo auto-cpufreq --install&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;imageblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;img src=&quot;auto_cpufreq_cycle.svg&quot; alt=&quot;auto cpufreq cycle&quot; width=&quot;189&quot; height=&quot;298&quot;&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect2&quot;&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;_thermald_automatic_thermal_protection&quot;&gt;thermald` : automatic thermal protection&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-bash hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;bash&quot;&gt;sudo apt install thermald
sudo systemctl enable thermald
sudo systemctl start thermald&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_real_time_monitoring&quot;&gt;Real-time monitoring&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect2&quot;&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;_sensors_on_the_command_line&quot;&gt;sensors` on the command line&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-bash hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;bash&quot;&gt;watch -n 2 sensors&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect2&quot;&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;_gui_option_psensor&quot;&gt;GUI option: `psensor&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-bash hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;bash&quot;&gt;sudo apt install psensor&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Simple graphical interface for CPU, GPU, fans.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_additional_tips&quot;&gt;Additional tips&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;ulist&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Close heavy tabs and applications (e.g., browsers)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Avoid intensive usage on battery&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Monitor the processes via`htop`:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-bash hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;bash&quot;&gt;sudo apt install htop
htop&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_clean_the_system_to_limit_overloads&quot;&gt;Clean the system to limit overloads&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-bash hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;bash&quot;&gt;sudo apt autoremove
sudo apt autoclean
sudo journalctl --vacuum-time=3d&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_to_go_further&quot;&gt;To go further&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;You can create a service`systemd`customized to automatically apply some settings at startup. Would you like an example in a future article? Let me know!&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_conclusion&quot;&gt;Conclusion&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;By combining these simple tools, you will gain:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;ulist&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A controlled CPU temperature&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Less ventilation noise&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Better autonomy&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Don&amp;#8217;t forget to monitor your temperatures regularly to prevent any overheating.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;__&lt;/em&gt; Better to prevent than to melt your CPU. &amp;lt;/think&amp;gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div id=&quot;toc&quot; class=&quot;toc&quot;&gt;
&lt;div id=&quot;toctitle&quot;&gt;Sommaire&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;ul class=&quot;sectlevel1&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#_the_battlefield_a_mockup_three_browsers_multiple_renderings&quot;&gt;The battlefield: a mockup, three browsers, multiple renderings&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#_problem_1_the_floating_card_of_the_hero_section&quot;&gt;Problem 1: The floating card of the &quot;Hero&quot; section&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#_problem_2_the_cannibal_navigation_bar&quot;&gt;Problem 2: The cannibal navigation bar&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#_problem_3_anarchic_typography&quot;&gt;Problem 3 : Anarchic typography&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#_problem_4_the_navigation_bar_that_overflows&quot;&gt;Problem 4: The navigation bar that overflows&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#_problem_5_the_invisible_hamburger_menu&quot;&gt;Problem 5: The invisible hamburger menu&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#_conclusion_the_lessons_of_relentless_debugging&quot;&gt;Conclusion: the lessons of relentless debugging&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div id=&quot;preamble&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;admonitionblock note&quot;&gt;
&lt;table&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;icon&quot;&gt;
&lt;i class=&quot;fa icon-note&quot; title=&quot;Note&quot;&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Reading time :&lt;/strong&gt; about 7 minutes &lt;strong&gt;Level :&lt;/strong&gt; Intermediate&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In this detailed case study, we will dissect the most common and sometimes most frustrating browser compatibility issues encountered when creating a UI mockup with Bootstrap 5.2. From handling Flexbox to the subtlety of font rendering, discover our debugging journey to turn a shaky design under Firefox into a pixel-perfect experience across all browsers.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_the_battlefield_a_mockup_three_browsers_multiple_renderings&quot;&gt;The battlefield: a mockup, three browsers, multiple renderings&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Every front-end developer knows this feeling. After hours of work, the mockup is finally perfect. Alignments are crisp, typography is elegant, animations are smooth. You let out a sigh of satisfaction, you admire your work on Chrome (or Brave, or Edge), then, just to be safe, you open the project on Firefox. And there, it&amp;#8217;s drama. Elements overflowing, broken alignments, fonts with chaotic sizes… the beautiful harmony has flown away.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It&amp;#8217;s precisely the scenario we faced when developing a new portfolio interface. The brief was simple: a modern, responsive home page, with a theme selector (light, dark, and high contrast), using vanilla JavaScript and the latest version of Bootstrap 5.2.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This article is not a list of miracle solutions. It is the logbook of our debugging session, a deep dive into the &quot;why&quot; of rendering differences between the Blink (Chromium, Brave) and Gecko (Firefox) engines, and the demonstration that robust, modern solutions are always better than hasty patches.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;imageblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;img src=&quot;./images/usecase-debugging-firefox.svg&quot; alt=&quot;usecase debugging firefox&quot; width=&quot;955&quot; height=&quot;352&quot;&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;title&quot;&gt;Figure 1. Use Case Diagram: The debugging process&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;imageblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;img src=&quot;./images/components-solution-architecture.svg&quot; alt=&quot;components solution architecture&quot; width=&quot;1286&quot; height=&quot;188&quot;&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;title&quot;&gt;Figure 2. Component Diagram: Architecture of the front-end solution&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_problem_1_the_floating_card_of_the_hero_section&quot;&gt;Problem 1: The floating card of the &quot;Hero&quot; section&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The first bug was also the most visible. The welcome section (&quot;hero&quot;) is composed of two columns: on the left, the main title; on the right, a presentation card.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;ulist&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The symptom:&lt;/strong&gt;On Chrome and Brave, the two columns were perfectly vertically centered. On Firefox, the right card inexplicably fell below the left text.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The investigation:&lt;/strong&gt;The HTML code, however, seemed simple and correct, using Bootstrap&amp;#8217;s standard classes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-html hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;html&quot;&gt;&amp;lt;section id=&quot;home&quot; class=&quot;hero-section&quot;&amp;gt;
    &amp;lt;div class=&quot;container&quot;&amp;gt;
        &amp;lt;!-- Cette ligne est la clé du problème --&amp;gt;
        &amp;lt;div class=&quot;row align-items-center min-vh-100&quot;&amp;gt;
            &amp;lt;div class=&quot;col-lg-6&quot;&amp;gt;
                &amp;lt;!-- Contenu de gauche --&amp;gt;
            &amp;lt;/div&amp;gt;
            &amp;lt;div class=&quot;col-lg-6&quot;&amp;gt;
                &amp;lt;!-- Carte de droite --&amp;gt;
            &amp;lt;/div&amp;gt;
        &amp;lt;/div&amp;gt;
    &amp;lt;/div&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;/section&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Our custom CSS for`.hero-section`was defining`display: flex` et &lt;code&gt;align-items: center&lt;/code&gt;, and the class`min-vh-100`gave it a minimum height to the line (&lt;code&gt;div.row&lt;/code&gt;). So why was Firefox refusing to center the columns?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;ulist&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The root cause :&lt;/strong&gt;It&amp;#8217;s a textbook case of how rendering engines interpret implied heights. The`&amp;lt;section&amp;gt;`had a`min-height`, but no`height`explicit. To apply`align-items-center`inside`div.row`, Firefox needs to know the reference height of this container. Like its parents (&lt;code&gt;div.container&lt;/code&gt; et la `&amp;lt;section&amp;gt;`herself) had no height&lt;strong&gt;strict&lt;/strong&gt;, Firefox was lost. The Blink engine, more permissive, manages to &quot;guess&quot; the intention and to center the elements. Gecko, more faithful to the specification, does not do that.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The solution:&lt;/strong&gt;Make the height explicit. We forced the`.container` et le `.row`to take up 100 % of the height of their respective parent using the utility class`h-100`of Bootstrap.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-html hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;html&quot;&gt;&amp;lt;section id=&quot;home&quot; class=&quot;hero-section&quot;&amp;gt;
    &amp;lt;div class=&quot;container h-100&quot;&amp;gt;
        &amp;lt;div class=&quot;row align-items-center min-vh-100 h-100&quot;&amp;gt;
            &amp;lt;!-- ... --&amp;gt;
        &amp;lt;/div&amp;gt;
    &amp;lt;/div&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;/section&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;admonitionblock tip&quot;&gt;
&lt;table&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;icon&quot;&gt;
&lt;i class=&quot;fa icon-tip&quot; title=&quot;Tip&quot;&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;When a`align-items`Flexbox does not work as expected on the vertical axis, always check that the container has a height (&lt;code&gt;height&lt;/code&gt; ou &lt;code&gt;min-height&lt;/code&gt;) defined and, if the problem persists (especially on Firefox), make sure that the intermediate parent containers properly propagate this height.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_problem_2_the_cannibal_navigation_bar&quot;&gt;Problem 2: The cannibal navigation bar&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;ulist&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;title&quot;&gt;Once the &quot;Hero&quot; section is aligned, another problem appeared: the navigation bar, with its class`fixed-top`, overlapped the title. A`padding-top: 80px`had been applied to the &quot;Hero&quot; section as a solution.&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The symptom:&lt;/strong&gt; Le `padding`was sufficient on Chrome, but not on Firefox, where the title was always partially hidden.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The root cause:&lt;/strong&gt;Using a &quot;magic number&quot; (&lt;code&gt;80px&lt;/code&gt;) is a very bad practice. The height of an element such as a navigation bar can vary by a few pixels from one browser to another due to subtle differences in font rendering, spacing, or even user settings. Relying on a fixed value is a recipe for a fragile design.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The solution:&lt;/strong&gt;A dynamic and foolproof solution in JavaScript. We wrote a small script to:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Measure the &lt;strong&gt;actual&lt;/strong&gt; height of the navigation bar after rendering the page.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Apply this measured height as`padding-top`to the Hero section.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Re-execute this function on every window resize to adapt to changes (such as switching to the hamburger menu).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-javascript hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;javascript&quot;&gt;document.addEventListener(&#39;DOMContentLoaded&#39;, function() {
    function adjustHeroPadding() {
        const navbar = document.querySelector(&#39;.navbar.fixed-top&#39;);
        const heroSection = document.querySelector(&#39;.hero-section&#39;);

        if (navbar &amp;amp;&amp;amp; heroSection) {
            const navbarHeight = navbar.offsetHeight;
            heroSection.style.paddingTop = navbarHeight + &#39;px&#39;;
        }
    }

    // Ajuster au chargement et au redimensionnement
    adjustHeroPadding();
    window.addEventListener(&#39;resize&#39;, adjustHeroPadding);
});&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;imageblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;img src=&quot;./images/sequence-dynamic-padding.svg&quot; alt=&quot;sequence dynamic padding&quot; width=&quot;887&quot; height=&quot;703&quot;&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;title&quot;&gt;Figure 3. Sequence Diagram: Dynamic padding adjustment&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In parallel, of course we have removed the rule`padding-top: 80px;`of our file`styles.css`.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_problem_3_anarchic_typography&quot;&gt;Problem 3 : Anarchic typography&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;ulist&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;title&quot;&gt;The following concern was the most damaging to the brand image: the size of the main title.&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The symptom:&lt;/strong&gt;On Firefox, the font of the title &quot;Développeur Formateur&amp;#8230;&amp;#8203;&quot; was gigantic, almost shocking, whereas it was harmonious on the other browsers.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Root cause:&lt;/strong&gt;The title used a class`display-4`of Bootstrap. These classes use responsive font sizes, often based on the unit`rem`and media queries. Once again, Firefox&amp;#8217;s rendering algorithm, combined with the &quot;Inter&quot; font, resulted in a much larger size calculation on our 1920x1080 resolution.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The solution:&lt;/strong&gt;Take back control with the CSS function`clamp()&lt;code&gt;. This function is a game-changer for fluid typography. It allows you to define a font size with three values: a minimum size, a &quot;preferred&quot; size (which adapts to the viewport width,`vw&lt;/code&gt;), and a maximum size.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-css hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;css&quot;&gt;.hero-content h1 {
    color: var(--text-primary);
    line-height: 1.2;
    /*
       Min: 2rem
       Préférée: s&#39;adapte à la vue
       Max: 2.5rem (40px)
    */
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 1.2rem + 1.5vw, 2.5rem);
}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With`clamp()&lt;code&gt;, we were able to tell the browser: &quot;Make the font grow with the screen, but do not exceed&lt;strong&gt;never&lt;/strong&gt;the limit of`2.5rem&lt;/code&gt;. This instantly harmonized the rendering across all browsers, giving us absolute control over the final aesthetic.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_problem_4_the_navigation_bar_that_overflows&quot;&gt;Problem 4: The navigation bar that overflows&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It was the problem that gave us the most trouble and ultimately revealed the deep nature of the rendering micro-differences.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;ulist&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The symptom:&lt;/strong&gt;On a 1920×1080 screen, the last links in the menu (&quot;Blog&quot;, &quot;Contact&quot;) were pushed off‑screen to the right, but only in Firefox.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Profound cause :&lt;/strong&gt;After several unsuccessful attempts (changing Bootstrap breakpoints of`lg` à &lt;code&gt;xl`then`xxl&lt;/code&gt;), we understood. The cause was not the Bootstrap logic, but a simple width calculation. On Firefox, the sum of the widths of all the links, including their`padding` et `margin`To the nearest sub-pixel, it was &lt;strong&gt;slightly&lt;/strong&gt; greater than 1920 pixels. On Chrome, this same sum was &lt;strong&gt;slightly&lt;/strong&gt; less. We were missing a few pixels.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The solution:&lt;/strong&gt;A surgical reduction of spacing. Since it was out of the question to specifically target Firefox with outdated CSS &quot;hacks&quot;, the only clean solution was to find a common style that works everywhere. So we slightly reduced the horizontal margins and padding of each navigation link.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-css hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;css&quot;&gt;/* La version finale, légèrement plus compacte */
.navbar-nav .nav-link {
    font-size: 0.9rem; /* 90% de la taille de base */
    color: var(--text-primary) !important;
    font-weight: 500;
    margin: 0 0.2rem;
    padding: 0.5rem 0.5rem !important;
    border-radius: 0.375rem;
    transition: all 0.3s ease;
}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This reduction, virtually invisible to the naked eye on a single element, collectively gave us the necessary space for all links to fit within the frame in Firefox, while preserving an almost identical appearance on other browsers.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_problem_5_the_invisible_hamburger_menu&quot;&gt;Problem 5: The invisible hamburger menu&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The last problem, and by no means the least, concerned mobile accessibility.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;ulist&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The symptom:&lt;/strong&gt;In responsive mode (hamburger menu), the menu icon was invisible on the &#39;dark&#39; and &#39;high-contrast&#39; themes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The root cause:&lt;/strong&gt;The icon of the Bootstrap 5 menu is a`background-image`(a URL-encoded SVG). By default, the color of its stroke is dark, optimized for a light background. It does not automatically adapt to theme changes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The solution:&lt;/strong&gt;Use Bootstrap CSS variables to override the icon. We have defined a new SVG icon, but this time with a white stroke.&lt;code&gt;#ffffff&lt;/code&gt;), and we applied it specifically when the themes`dark` ou `high-contrast`are active.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlightjs highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-css hljs&quot; data-lang=&quot;css&quot;&gt;/* ======================
   Fix pour l&#39;icône du menu Burger (Blanc)
   ====================== */
[data-bs-theme=&quot;dark&quot;] .navbar-toggler-icon,
[data-bs-theme=&quot;high-contrast&quot;] .navbar-toggler-icon {
    --bs-navbar-toggler-icon-bg: url(&quot;data:image/svg+xml,%3csvg xmlns=&#39;http://www.w3.org/2000/svg&#39; viewBox=&#39;0 0 30 30&#39;%3e%3cpath stroke=&#39;%23ffffff&#39; stroke-linecap=&#39;round&#39; stroke-miterlimit=&#39;10&#39; stroke-width=&#39;2&#39; d=&#39;M4 7h22M4 15h22M4 23h22&#39;/%3e%3c/svg%3e&quot;);
}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;admonitionblock important&quot;&gt;
&lt;table&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;icon&quot;&gt;
&lt;i class=&quot;fa icon-important&quot; title=&quot;Important&quot;&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Customizing Bootstrap components like this one is increasingly done via overriding CSS variables (&lt;code&gt;--bs-component-property&lt;/code&gt;), which is the cleanest and most sustainable method.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sect1&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;_conclusion_the_lessons_of_relentless_debugging&quot;&gt;Conclusion: the lessons of relentless debugging&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;sectionbody&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This journey, although sometimes frustrating, is rich in lessons. Each solved problem reinforces a fundamental truth of web development:&lt;strong&gt;Nothing replaces a rigorous cross-browser test.&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;imageblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;img src=&quot;./images/mindmap-debugging-summary.svg&quot; alt=&quot;mindmap debugging summary&quot; width=&quot;999&quot; height=&quot;866&quot;&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;title&quot;&gt;Figure 4. Mindmap : Summary of debugging and lessons learned&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;olist arabic&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;title&quot;&gt;These are the lessons we retain:&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;ol class=&quot;arabic&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Firefox is an ally:&lt;/strong&gt;Its greater fidelity to CSS standards forces us to write code that is more robust and less ambiguous.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Avoid the &quot;magic numbers&quot; :&lt;/strong&gt;Fixed values (such as`padding-top: 80px`) are ticking time bombs. Always prefer dynamic solutions (JavaScript) or relative ones (Flexbox, Grid).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Master your typography:&lt;/strong&gt;Use`clamp()`for full control over the size of responsive fonts.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Think in &quot;components&quot;:&lt;/strong&gt;To customize Bootstrap, override its CSS variables instead of adding many rules that override the framework.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Do not target a browser:&lt;/strong&gt;The solution is almost never to make a \&quot;hack\&quot; for a specific browser, but to find a common style that works everywhere.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Finally, the mockup is now solid, predictable, and ready to be integrated into a templating system. Every bug fixed was not a failure, but a step towards better quality code.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div id=&quot;toc&quot; class=&quot;toc&quot;&gt;
