&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I don&amp;#8217;t feel like installing.https://www.pgadmin.org/[pgAdmin] ou &lt;a href=&quot;http://phppgadmin.sourceforge.net/&quot;&gt;phppgadmin&lt;/a&gt; while everything is present on the command line.&lt;br&gt;
For that we need to launch the terminal and log in as the postgres user.&lt;br&gt;
The postgres user is the only one who by default has the right to launch the psql application, which is the command-line client. &amp;lt;/think&amp;gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interface_en_ligne_de_commande&quot;&gt;CLI&lt;/a&gt;) postgresql,&lt;br&gt;
to be able to talk to the database from the command line in the terminal&lt;br&gt;
To connect, launch the terminal and type this:&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-bash&quot; data-lang=&quot;bash&quot;&gt;$ sudo -i -u postgres&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To connect and go directly to the PostgreSQL command prompt, type this:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;listingblock&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-bash&quot; data-lang=&quot;bash&quot;&gt;$ sudo -i -u postgres psql&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;paragraph&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To list the databases of a PostgreSQL instance, here is the command:&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
